// =============================================================================
// core.lumen — the Beads Protocol (BDP) Core, authored as Lumen
// =============================================================================
//
// This single Lumen source is the Core spec. The toolchain projects it
// deterministically to the JSON Schema bundle and the OpenAPI catalog (both
// shipped as part of this specification), plus the generated Markdown reference,
// Go interfaces, and the canonical CLI. The Markdown under specs/generated/ is
// generated from this file, never hand-written.
//
// Source of record: gastownhall/bdp @ main. Owner: the spec owner (Lumen + BDP).
// Version: 0.2.3 — see specs/src/CHANGELOG.md. Pre-1.0 draft; the changelog marks
// what moved so an implementer tracking the spec in parallel can diff against it.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What BDP is
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// BDP is a refinement of HTTP (RFC 2616) and REST. The declarations below are a
// convenient surface syntax that lowers deterministically to JSON Schema and
// OpenAPI. A BDP service is a REST service; the forms here add, as OPTIONAL
// convention over REST:
//   - provenance            — who changed a resource, how, and when.
//   - revision history      — every resource is versioned; prior revisions are addressable.
//   - streaming             — one convention for reading a sequence: the three pull forms
//                             (windowed · bulk · live) over a single cursor.
//   - export & change notification — one representation serves both the bulk backup
//                             (export) and the live changefeed.
//   - an overlay system     — types, operations, and channels described as resources,
//                             so any BDP implementation can distribute them.
//   - a collection          — a standard library of useful types, operations, and channels
//                             (a separate spec, layered on core).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §0. Design decisions (current — the newer ruling wins)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Recorded as decisions so the design does not read as drift. These reflect the
// model closed 2026-11-16; where an older dated ruling conflicts, this list wins
// (the archaeology lives in git history).
//
//  1. nominal types on the Lumen surface. Every named shape is a `type` with
//     identity — the protocol is a vocabulary of named concepts, not structural
//     shorthand. (`schema` — transparent, structural — is for app modeling, not
//     this spec.) on the wire a Resource still carries only `type` (a path);
//     identity is a source-language concern, erased on the wire.
//  2. one reference world. `ref<T>` (a typed, phantom `ResourceId`) is the only
//     way one resource names another; the pin lives in `rev`. `AbsoluteUri`
//     survives only for non-ref URIs (a Problem `type`, a Principal `id`).
//  3. `ref<T>` is by-reference; `T` itself is by-value — the representation a read
//     returns. `get` turns a `ref<T>` into a `T`.
//  4. `kind` is NOT stored — it is `type.id`, the single-`is` refinement path
//     (`type Task is Issue` ⇒ "issue/task"). Single inheritance is what makes the
//     path a unique tree.
//  5. A `Resource { fields }` refinement puts the subtype's fields into `body` —
//     the firewall is the default: `Resource { fields } ≡ Resource<XBody>`. A
//     "bead" is just a Resource whose authority is a package; content is a
//     Resource with a media-type `type` and a raw `body`.
//  6. `union` is for closed value-level sum types only; kind hierarchies are open
//     single-`is` refinement.
//  7. Dispatch is implicit — a request routes to its target resource (a bead takes
//     CRUD, an Operation takes `call`, a Channel takes `pull`/`push`). No dispatch table.
//  8. error model. A data primitive returns its success value directly and faults
//     with a Problem (an HTTP status). `Result<T,E>` / `Outcome<T,E>` are `call`'s
//     alone — the declared success/failure of a user Operation. Writes return a
//     pinned ref; reads return a resource; `delete` returns the tombstone Rev.
//  9. The primitives are a subset a service may offer, with `get` and discovery the
//     mandatory floor; discovery advertises which others, and how each behaves.
//     Built-in data primitives are NOT Operations.
// 10. `committedAt` and `inception` are server-supplied on write, never request
//     input; a Request's `provenance` is the caller's part only.
//
// open / parked (not in this file): the scheduler (deadline, timeout, graceful
// cancel + compensation); the `/sys/` scheme beyond `/sys/changes`; capability
// discovery; full filter / query / search / count / graph (corelib capabilities,
// advertised, not base); Transfer; the `canceled` HTTP status;
// the deferred types pass (BeadSchema ⇄ PackageElementRef).
//
// Review markers: a line beginning `>DB?` is an open question for the owner;
// `// TYPES-PASS:` marks work deferred to the type-value reconciliation.
// =============================================================================



// #############################################################################
// 1. Resources
// #############################################################################
// A BDP store holds versioned resources. A resource has an identity, a type, and
// a body; you name one with `ref<T>` and fetch it as a `T`. This section builds
// that vocabulary bottom-up: the id decoder ring (scope) and version tokens,
// identity, the reference/representation pair (and `get`), revision pinning, the
// universal Resource shape, the authority/type pair, then metadata and provenance.

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.1  Identity — the id decoder ring (scope), revision tokens, ResourceId
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Read this first. Every resource is named by an id, and `scope` is the decoder
// ring that says how to read that id. The revision tokens and the assembled
// ResourceId follow from it.

## BdpScope
/// description: The URL of a BDP scope (a store).
type BdpScope = AbsoluteUri

## Scope
/// description: The decoder ring for every id — the namespace an `id` resolves in, and so how to read the `id`. The one place the three readings are defined; everything that carries an id is read through this.
/// normative: A Scope is exactly one of —
///   • `null` (equivalently, absent on the wire): the local BDP scope — `id` is a local name in the store you are talking to.
///   • a BdpScope URL: another BDP store — `id` is relative to that URL (the wire URL is the scope URL ⊕ id).
///   • the literal `"uri"`: NOT a BDP scope — `id` is itself an absolute URI, resolved by its scheme.
type Scope =
    null
  | BdpScope
  | "uri"

## Rev
/// description: A resource-version token — opaque by definition; compared for exact equality only, never inspected.
/// normative: A `Rev` is opaque: compared for equality only, never parsed or inspected.
/// normative: The revision changes exactly when the resource's state changes; a write that would leave the state unchanged mints no new revision (and produces no event).
type Rev is string

## Digest
/// description: A content-addressed rev (sha256 / commit) — a Rev that is a digest.
/// normative: `Digest is Rev`, so a digest satisfies any `rev: Rev`; there is no Rev-vs-Digest split on the wire.
type Digest is Rev

## ResourceId
/// description: The identity of a resource — a scope, an id within it, and an optional revision. The wire URL is the projection (scope URL ⊕ id).
type ResourceId {
  /// description: How to read the `id` — see [[core.lumen#Scope]], the decoder ring. Absent on the wire ≡ the `null` arm (local).
  scope?: Scope
  /// description: The id, relative to `scope` (or the absolute URI when `scope` is `"uri"`).
  id:     string
  /// description: The specific revision; absent ⇒ the current one.
  rev?:   Rev
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.2  References & representations — ref<T>, rep<T>, and `get`
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// You name a resource with a `ref<T>` and read it into a `rep<T>` — the
// representation (the envelope around a `body: T`, §1.4). `get` turns the first
// into the second. `T` is the resource's BODY type: `ref<Issue>` references the
// resource whose body is `Issue`.

## ResourceRef
/// description: A reference to a resource is just its id.
type ResourceRef = ResourceId

## ref
/// description: A typed (phantom) reference — the by-reference form. `T` is the referenced resource's BODY type; it is checker-only and erases on the wire (a `ref` is a `ResourceId`).
type ref<T> = ResourceRef

## get
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "get" }
/// description: Safe point read — resolve a `ref<T>` to the current representation `rep<T>`, or a pinned revision when the ref pins `rev` (or `?revision=`). An optional `selector` projects the result (which gets its own cacheable identity). Faults 404/410 when absent or erased.
/// normative: `get` is safe and has no declared failure — it returns a `rep<T>` (the by-value representation of the referenced resource) or faults with a Problem. A `selector`-projected read is a partial representation, cacheable under its own query-string identity.
/// normative: A resource outside the caller's authorization view is reported exactly as a missing one — identical status, on every operation and every stream. A service never reveals that a hidden resource exists, including through an error only a hidden resource could cause.
/// openapi = { operationId = "get", method = "GET", pathItem = "resource", tags = ["Core"], params = { revision = { in = "query", schema = "Rev", required = false }, include = { in = "query", schema = "[JsonPointer]", required = false }, omit = { in = "query", schema = "[JsonPointer]", required = false }, expand = { in = "query", schema = "[LocalName]", required = false }, select = { in = "query", schema = "selector", required = false } }, success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [404,410] } }
fn get<T>(
    /// description: The reference to resolve — current revision, or the pinned one when `rev` is set (or `?revision=`).
    resource:  ref<T>,
    /// description: Optional projection; a projected read gets its own cacheable identity.
    selector?: selector
  ) -> rep<T>

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.3  Revision pinning — pinned<T> / unpinned<T>
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Every resource is versioned; a reference either pins one exact revision or
// floats to the current one.

## pinned
/// description: A reference pinned to one exact revision (`rev` present).
/// normative: A pin is a local Rev or an external Digest; since `Digest is Rev`, both satisfy `rev: Rev`.
type pinned<T>   is ref<T> { rev: Rev }

## unpinned
/// description: An unpinned reference (`rev` absent) — floats to the current revision.
type unpinned<T> is ref<T> { rev: null }

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.4  The representation — rep<T> — and the kind markers
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A resource splits cleanly in two: a uniform protocol ENVELOPE and a typed BODY.
// `rep<T>` is the on-wire REPRESENTATION — the envelope around a `body: T`. A
// domain type (`Issue`, `TypeDef`, `rel<…>`) is written as a plain record — the
// body ALONE — and the envelope is applied by `rep<T>` at the point of use:
//     type Issue is Bead { title: string, status: string }   // Issue IS the body
//     get(ref<Issue>) -> rep<Issue>                           // { id, …, body: Issue }
// There is no firewall rule and no envelope-field magic: the fields a type writes
// ARE its body, and nothing nests them — `rep<T>` does, explicitly, once. So the
// wire is root-addressed with no remapping: a selector / filter / patch names
// `/body/status`, `/type`, `/metadata/…` literally. The CLI MAY sugar the `/body`
// prefix away; the wire does not.

## rep
/// description: The representation of a versioned resource — the protocol envelope around a typed `body: T`. The by-value form a read returns; `ref<T>` is the by-reference form. `Resource` is `rep<any>`, an untyped representation.
/// normative: The envelope members are fixed and closed — a domain type contributes only `body`, never a new envelope member. `id`, `type` and `body` are always present; the rest is optional. On a representation `id.rev` is the resolved revision; a reference floats to the current revision when its `rev` is absent.
type rep<T> = {
  /// description: The resource's identity (scope + id); on a representation `rev` is the resolved revision, on a reference the optional pin.
  id:            ResourceId
  /// description: Who names `type` — a package (bead) or a registry like IANA (content). Absent ⇒ ad-hoc / duck-typed.
  authority?:    Authority
  /// description: The type path, resolved within `authority`'s namespace.
  type:          PathString
  /// description: The typed body — the domain payload. `any` for an untyped `Resource`; a record for a bead; raw bytes/text for a content resource.
  body:          T
  /// description: Opaque per-resource sidecar the protocol does not interpret.
  metadata?:     Metadata
  /// description: Who caused this revision, how its period began, and when.
  provenance?:   Provenance
  /// description: The resource's birth time, denormalized onto every revision.
  createdAt?:    Timestamp
  /// description: How the resource is kept — durable, ephemeral, or intrinsic.
  storageClass?: StorageClass
}

## Resource
/// description: An untyped representation — `rep<any>`. Used where the body type is unknown or open (a mixed listing, a generic read).
type Resource = rep<any>

## Bead
/// description: A vacuous kind marker — a body type tagged `is Bead` is a package-authored record. It adds no members; it marks intention, lets a signature demand a bead (`ref<Bead>`), and carries the enforcement that a bead body is a record under a package authority. It is the implicit ROOT of a bead's kind path and contributes NO path segment: `type Issue is Bead` ⇒ kind `"issue"`.
type Bead {}

## Content
/// description: A vacuous kind marker — a body type tagged `is Content` is a registry-named (media-type) resource with an opaque body (bytes / text). The complement of `Bead`; a root marker, no path segment.
type Content {}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.5  Types on a resource — the (authority, type) pair
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A resource's type is a path, fully qualified by the authority that names it.
// (The overlay that defines types — TypeDef — is §2.1.)

## PathString
/// description: A slash-delimited path — `"issue/task"` (a bead kind) or `"text/plain"` (a content media type).
/// normative: Two first path segments are reserved — `bdp` (the protocol's own fixed routes and artifacts, e.g. `/bdp/openapi`) and `sys` (in-band system resources, e.g. `/sys/changes`).
/// normative: A bare `-` segment is reserved everywhere as the ACTION FENCE: in any path the segment `-` separates a (possibly slash-bearing) resource id on its left from a protocol action or projection on its right (`/{id}/-/revisions`, `/-/list`). The fence is matched on the RAW path, before percent-decoding, and is the LAST `/-/` in the path; the segment(s) to its right are validated against the closed, fence-free set of protocol actions (a right side that is not a known action faults `404`). No resource-id segment may be a bare `-`: a literal hyphen-only segment inside an id MUST be percent-encoded (`%2D`), and a write that would mint an id with an unencoded bare `-` segment is refused — so a crafted `%2D` never forges the fence and the id/action split is deterministic.
type PathString = string

## Authority
/// description: The resource that names a `type` — a package (for a bead) or a registry like IANA (for content). A ResourceRef, so the pin lives in its `rev`.
/// normative: The pair (authority, type) fully qualifies a type. The authority is pinned (its `rev` = the resolved digest/semver), so the type a resource was born against is frozen at inception and replays identically.
/// normative: `authority` MAY be caller-supplied (accepted as given); when absent on a write it is server-resolved from `type` — an installed default package (the standard types) is consulted before a registry like IANA, and a `type` that resolves to neither is ad-hoc (authority absent / duck-typed). A service SHOULD cache the well-known media types so content resolution need not reach IANA.
type Authority = ResourceRef

## PackageRef
/// description: A pinned reference to a Package resource — an Authority that is a package (a bead's authority).
type PackageRef = ref<PackageDef>

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.6  Metadata & provenance
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Metadata
/// description: Opaque, open per-resource (or per-call) sidecar — data the protocol does not interpret.
type Metadata = { ...: any }

## Provenance
/// description: Who took part in the change that made a revision, how the Resource's present period began, and when. `previousRevision` is the before-image pointer — the basis for compensation — and rides in the post-image, so a ChangeEvent carries it for free.
/// normative: Provenance is data, not evidence — Core does not claim it is true. It describes one change and is not copied forward (the sole exception is `inception`). `committedAt` and `inception` are server-supplied, never caller input; a request that changes nothing records none, and one request that mints several revisions records provenance on each. A service MUST NOT invent a principal to fill an absent role; a caller cannot supply `basis` (`channel-authenticated` is service-assigned only, and `authority` then names that service).
type Provenance = {
  /// description: The principals who took part in the change. May be empty for observed or baseline revisions.
  principals:        [ Principal ],
  /// description: How the Resource's present period began — freshly created, imported, or unknown. Under `permanent` a deleted identity is terminal and never reused; under `reusable` a retired id MAY be reminted per the scope's id model ([[core.lumen#IdModel]]).
  inception:         "create" | "import" | "unknown",
  /// description: When this revision committed; server-supplied, never request input.
  committedAt:       Timestamp,
  /// description: The before-image pointer — the basis for compensation. Null on create; structural lineage, often known even when the principals are not.
  previousRevision?: Rev,
  /// description: An optional free-text note describing the change.
  message?:          string,
  /// description: Per-revision opaque data (data, open).
  metadata?:         Metadata
}

## Principal
/// description: An identity that took part in a change — a person, agent or service account. A role grants no permission; authorization is a separate decision.
type Principal = {
  /// description: The principal's identity URI. Need not be resolvable.
  id:           AbsoluteUri,
  /// description: A saved human label — never identity, never a matching key.
  /// normative: `displayName` is never identity and never a matching/resolution key.
  displayName?: string,
  /// description: Open set of roles this principal played in the change (initiator, contributor, submitter, …). A role grants no permission.
  roles?:       [ string ],
  /// description: How the service established this identity.
  basis:        IdentityBasis,
  /// description: The service that channel-authenticated this identity.
  authority?:   AbsoluteUri
}

## IdentityBasis
/// description: How a service established a principal's identity.
/// normative: Open union — a reader MUST tolerate an unrecognized basis. New authentication bases are added without a breaking change.
type IdentityBasis = union {
  /// description: The writer asserted the identity; the service did not verify it.
  "writer-supplied"
  /// description: The channel authenticated the identity when the change arrived.
  "channel-authenticated"
  /// description: The basis is not recorded.
  "unknown"
}

## StorageClass
/// description: How a Resource is kept — whether it is persisted, not whether it is versioned (that is the scope's revision model). Durable is replicated and exported; ephemeral is local and never exported; intrinsic is computed by the service, not stored (capability beads are intrinsic).
/// normative: Export (the primitive) includes only `Durable` resources; `StorageClass` is per-resource, so a `Durable` scope MAY hold individual `Ephemeral` resources, and an `Ephemeral` resource is local state that is NEVER included in an export stream; `Intrinsic` is computed by the service, not stored.
/// normative: Whether revisions are kept is a SCOPE-WIDE property, not per-resource: a scope's `revisionModel` ([[core.lumen#RevisionModel]], advertised in discovery) is `versioned` (mints revisions; write refs `pinned`) or `unversioned` (mints none; write refs relax to `unpinned`). StorageClass is orthogonal — it says only whether a resource is persisted.
type StorageClass = union {
  /// description: Replicated and exported (persisted). Whether its revisions are kept is the scope's `revisionModel`, not a property of the resource.
  Durable
  /// description: Local and never exported.
  Ephemeral
  /// description: Computed by the service, not stored.
  Intrinsic
}

## Timestamp
/// description: RFC 3339 date-time; uppercase `T` and `Z`.
/// normative: A `Timestamp` is RFC 3339 with an uppercase `T` and `Z`.
/// jsonschema = { format = "date-time" }
type Timestamp is string

## AbsoluteUri
/// description: Any absolute URI — a Problem `type`, a Principal `id`; need not resolve. NOT a resource reference (those are `ref<T>`).
/// jsonschema = { format = "uri" }
type AbsoluteUri is string

## SemVer
/// description: A semantic-version string (a package's declared version).
type SemVer is string

## integer
/// description: A whole number — JSON `number` restricted to an integer value. Distinct from `number` (which admits fractions) so a typed or generated client maps it to an integer type (e.g. i64); on the wire it is an integer-valued JSON number. Use `integer` for counts, bounds, positions and priorities; keep `number` for genuinely fractional values (a score, a ratio).
/// jsonschema = { type = "integer" }
type integer is number

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.7  Relationships — a relationship is a resource
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A relationship between two resources is not a construct of its own: it is a
// Resource (a bead) whose body implicitly carries two endpoints and the
// relationship's own properties, with its own identity, revision history and
// provenance. There is no separate edge or link type. A relationship type is
// declared with the `rel<F, T>` form and refined with `is`, e.g.
// `type Dependency is unique acyclic rel<Issue, Issue> { note?: string }` and
// `type Blocks is Dependency`.

## rel
/// description: The relationship construct and its declaration form. `rel<F, T>` is a `Bead` body type that carries `source` (type F, the owning endpoint that asserts the relationship) and `target` (type T), plus the relationship type's own properties; its resource is `rep<rel<F, T>>`. A relationship type is declared `type X is <modifiers> rel<F, T> { <edge-fields> }` — a nominal refinement whose trailing record extends the body; a bare `<modifiers> rel<F, T>` is the same shape without the nominal identity. The declared name is the body type; `ref<X>` references the relationship, `rep<X>` is its representation.
/// normative: A relationship IS a resource (a bead — its authority is a package): it has its own identity, revision history and provenance. Body is implicit — `source`/`target` are not written, and the `{ <edge-fields> }` are ordinary body properties, not a second "edge metadata" bag. Changing a property mints a new revision of the relationship itself; creating or deleting a relationship does NOT touch either endpoint's revision. A relationship is a valid reference target, and its endpoints MAY themselves be relationships.
/// normative: Nothing distinguishes a relationship but its structural `source`/`target` pair — a bare `ref<T>` in any other body field is data, not a relationship; an edge is a declared relationship type, never an incidental reference. Referential integrity follows the KIND of reference: `source`/`target` are STRUCTURAL (a scope cascades an endpoint's retirement to the relationship, or restricts the retirement while it stands — a relationship never outlives a structural endpoint); a reference that only points AT a resource (a note, an annotation) is SOFT (it tolerates a retired target and resolves to its tombstone). Deletion is the resource tombstone ([[core.lumen#delete]]) — a terminal revision that keeps the id and history; a true destroy (purge) is a separate, rare operation, not a base primitive.
/// normative: `unique` is the optional uniqueness modifier — `unique rel<F, T>` keys the relationship by its natural key (`source`, `target`) and derives its id deterministically, so the same logical relationship resolves to one id in every replica. Without `unique`, each takes a surrogate id and several over the same pair coexist as distinct resources. The natural key is (concrete `type`, `source`, `target`): at most one relationship of a GIVEN concrete type per pair, while different concrete types over the same pair (e.g. `Blocks` and `Related` over A→B) coexist as distinct resources with distinct ids. Re-typing a relationship is therefore a delete + create (a new id), not a same-id mutation. Graph modifiers constrain the relation as a whole — `acyclic` (a DAG), `symmetric`, `reflexive`; the default is irreflexive and `acyclic` implies it. Reachability and traversal through relationships are a corelib surface, not Core.
type rel<F, T> is Bead { source: ref<F>, target: ref<T>, ...: any }

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.8  Computed properties — derived, and revision-transparent
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `computed` is a body-property modifier, NOT a type — it sits with the
// access-mode modifiers (`init-only` / `service-managed` / `read-only`). A field
// marked `computed` is DERIVED — a pure function of the store's authored state,
// not authored input — and carries one rule: a derived value leaves no trace in
// the revision stream. It never contributes to the revision (a change in a
// computed value alone mints no new Rev and emits no ChangeEvent — the
// state-unchanged rule already excludes it), it is excluded from any content
// hash, and it surfaces read-only (a write that sets it is ignored, never
// authored). Its storage is unspecified — recompute on read, or cache — provided
// the authoritative value stays the function of authored state and the cache
// never appears in the revision stream. Core declares only the category and this
// contract; the derivation itself is supplied by a pack, never by Core.
//
// > **Open** — the exact grammar by which `computed` and the access-mode
// > modifiers attach to a field is settled with that modifier family in the
// > Lumen scrub.



// #############################################################################
// 2. Beads
// #############################################################################
// A bead is a resource whose body is a package-defined record and whose authority
// is that package. A bead type is written as the BODY alone, tagged `is Bead`:
// `type Issue is Bead { title: string, status: string }` — `Issue` IS the body
// record, and its resource is `rep<Issue>` (§1.4). `Bead` is a vacuous kind marker
// (intention + static enforcement), the implicit root of the kind path, adding no
// path segment. "Content" (`is Content` — a media-type `type`, an opaque `body`)
// is the complement; the marker also carries the authority intention (a package
// ref ⇒ bead; a registry ref like IANA ⇒ content), and `authority` is optional, so
// a bead MAY be duck-typed. The four definitional bead kinds (Type, Channel,
// Operation, Package) are how BDP describes and distributes types, operations, and
// channels — the overlay system; each is a `Bead` body type whose resource is a
// `rep<…>`, with no separate `…Bead` wrapper type.

// Content is a bare Resource — no narrowing: `type` is a media type named by a
// registry, `body` is raw bytes/text. (See the worked examples at the end.)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.9  Aliases — a second name that overlays the scope root
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// An alias is a second name for a resource. It is itself a resource — an `Alias`
// bead parked in the reserved `sys/aliases/` namespace — so it is bound, read,
// repointed and removed with the ordinary primitives, and keeps its own history.
// No alias-specific operation exists. Aliases are present only when the scope
// advertises the `aliases` capability ([[core.lumen#AliasCaps]]).

## Alias
/// description: A named pointer — a second name for a resource. The alias's own id is the name, under the reserved `sys/aliases/` root; its `target` names the resource the name stands for.
/// normative: An `Alias` is an ordinary Bead at `sys/aliases/{name}`. It is managed entirely through the existing primitives — `put` binds or repoints it, `delete` removes it, `get` reads it, and `list` over `sys/aliases` (filtered on `target`) enumerates the names that point at a resource. Repointing mints a revision, so an alias carries its own history. `target` is a SOFT reference: a deleted target is not rewritten, so an unpinned alias then resolves to the target's tombstone and faults `410 Gone`.
/// normative: THE OVERLAY (resolution, not an operation). When the scope advertises `aliases`, id resolution gains one step: a bare scope-root id that names an `Alias` resolves THROUGH it to the alias's `target` before the operation runs. So `get`, `put`, `patch` and `delete` on an alias name act on the TARGET, and a read returns the target's `id`/`rev` — the alias leaves no trace in the result. The primitives are unchanged; only id resolution carries the extra hop. The binding itself is addressed directly at `sys/aliases/{name}`, never through the overlay, so `delete sys/aliases/{name}` removes the name while `delete {name}` deletes the target. Because a dereferenced read never reveals that a name was an alias, the two interrogations are explicit: a `get` on `sys/aliases/{name}` (present ⇒ it is an alias) and the filtered `list` (the names that point at a target).
/// normative: SINGLE HOP, SAME SCOPE. `target` names a resource in the alias's own scope and MUST NOT name another `Alias` — binding an alias whose target is a live alias is refused `invalid-target`, so chains and cycles are unrepresentable. The overlay resolves by TRANSPARENT REWRITE (no `3xx`): `GET {name}` is served as `GET {target}` and the reply carries `Content-Location: /{target}` naming the target's canonical id (a cache still keys on the request target URI, not on this header; an unsafe request invalidates the named URI per RFC 9111 §4.4). The reverse lookup — `list sys/aliases` filtered on `target` — matches `target` against the canonical id string.
/// normative: UNIQUENESS. A scope-root name is held by AT MOST ONE claimant — a resource OR an alias, never both. `create` (or an id-rooted `put`) at a name that is a live alias is refused `identity-taken`; binding an `Alias` whose name is a live resource id is refused likewise; and the id a server mints for an unrequested `create` avoids live alias names exactly as it avoids live ids. The guarantee spans both claim paths at once.
type Alias is Bead {
  /// description: The resource this name stands for — a SOFT, SINGLE-HOP reference to a resource in the SAME scope; never another alias (no chains) and never a foreign scope. Tolerates a retired target (resolves to its tombstone).
  target: ref<any>
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2.1  Types — the kind system, TypeDef & the value-schema
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// path = chain. Bead subtypes use single inheritance; the `is` chain builds the
// element path, which is `type.id`, which is `kind`:
//   type Issue is Bead  { … }   → "issue"
//   type Task  is Issue { … }   → "issue/task"
// Only one `is` parent is allowed — that is what makes the path (a tree) unique.
// A kind / type-path PREFIX test is SEGMENT-bounded: a prefix matches only at a `/`
// boundary (or the whole path), so `issue` matches `issue` and `issue/task` but NOT
// `issuecomment`. Naive string-prefix matching false-matches siblings — a real bug,
// and it governs every capability query (`dependency/ready-affecting/*`) and `is-a` test.
// A segment is the type NAME kebab-cased (PascalCase/camelCase → kebab): `Issue` → "issue",
// `IssueComment` → "issue-comment", `HardBlocking` → "hard-blocking", `ReadyAffecting` →
// "ready-affecting" — matching the dependency types and the cli-flag convention; NOT
// lowercase-run-together ("issuecomment" is wrong).
//
// inheritance (how a chain flattens to one effective schema):
//   - additive: a child has all parent fields plus its own
//   - narrowing-only override: narrow a type, or optional→required; never widen or
//     drop — preserves substitutability (ref<Issue> may hold a Task)
//   - a same-named child field must be a narrowing, else a diagnostic
//   - `abstract?` on a TypeDef ⇒ not directly instantiable (a bead must use a leaf)
//
// resolver (Types-layer capability, NOT core): walk the pinned path base→leaf and
// merge per the rules — the path is the plan. A pinned authority ⇒ the effective
// schema is immutable and cacheable. A cross-package parent is a `refines` ref the
// resolver follows across authorities.
//
// cross-module: a foreign name never enters the path. A cross-module `is` makes the
// child a local root whose `refines` holds a resolved ref (identity carries a ref,
// not a name). Within a module kind == schema-ancestry; across modules kind stays
// local and "is-a" is a `refines`-graph query (a capability), not a kind-prefix scan.
//
// refinement vs union: refinement (`is`) = open kind hierarchy, path-addressed;
// `union` = closed value-level sum type.

## TypeDef
/// description: A type-definition bead body — one node in the single-`is` refinement tree. Its resource is `rep<TypeDef>`.
/// normative: A type's element path is parent-path ⊕ its own name. A same-package parent is the path prefix (implicit); `refines` is set only for a cross-package parent. The Types resolver flattens the chain base→leaf per the §2.1 inheritance rules.
type TypeDef is Bead {
  /// description: Cross-package parent only; a same-package parent is the path prefix. Null + no prefix ⇒ root.
  refines?:   TypeRef
  /// description: True ⇒ not directly instantiable; a bead must use a leaf.
  /// normative: An `abstract` type is not directly instantiable; a bead MUST use a concrete (leaf) type.
  abstract?:  bool
  /// description: The body properties of resources of this type; inherited properties merge per the §2.1 rules.
  properties: [ PropertyDeclaration ]
}

## PropertyDeclaration
/// description: One property in a type's body — its name, its type, and its access mode. `computed` marks a derived, revision-transparent property (see §1.8); `initOnly` / `serviceManaged` / `readOnly` are the sibling write-access modes. Property optionality rides the property's `type`.
/// normative: At most one access mode applies. `computed` ⇒ derived and revision-transparent (never authored, never advances the revision, hash-excluded, surfaces read-only). `initOnly` ⇒ writable only at create. `serviceManaged` ⇒ set by the service, never a writer. `readOnly` ⇒ not writable through this surface. All absent ⇒ an ordinary authored property. A read-only member (`computed`, `serviceManaged` or `readOnly`) supplied on a write (`create` / `put` / `patch`) is IGNORED, never faulted — so a `get` → `put` round-trip of a whole representation is safe.
type PropertyDeclaration = {
  name:            string,
  type:            BeadSchema,
  computed?:       bool,
  initOnly?:       bool,
  // Open (Lumen scrub): do `serviceManaged` / `readOnly` earn their place, or does `computed` subsume them?
  serviceManaged?: bool,
  readOnly?:       bool
}

## ParameterDeclaration
/// description: One parameter of an operation — its name, its type, and whether it is required. A default value is NOT a core concern; it is a CLI surface hint carried outside the protocol.
type ParameterDeclaration = {
  name:      string,
  type:      BeadSchema,
  required?: bool
}

## BeadSchema
/// description: A type written down as a value — the ref-preserving type representation. (A `schema` has no identity, so a type-as-value is a `schema`.)
/// normative: On export a BeadSchema lowers to a JSON Schema document (and an OpenAPI contract) for external consumers; the exported/wire form is byte-identical to the pre-BeadSchema JSON Schema, so the v0.2.0 freeze is NOT reopened. Only genuinely open data uses `{ ...: any }`; a written-down type uses this.
// TYPES-PASS: the whole of BeadSchema is the deferred type-value reconciliation.
//   - `TypeRef { to: ... }` must align to `PackageElementRef` (authority? + path); `Kind` is gone.
//   - renamed BeadValueSchema → BeadSchema. Still open: do we need the `Nominal`
//     arm? · `Opaque` → `JsonSchema { text }`?
//     directly? · is `LocalName` needed, and if so why isn't it the type of a DU tag?
type BeadSchema =
    Scalar  { of: "string" | "number" | "bool" | "bytes" }
  | Literal { value: any }
  | Nominal { name: string, base: BeadSchema, refinement?: { ...: any } }
  | Record  { fields: [ { name: string, type: BeadSchema, required: bool } ], open: bool }
  | DU      { arms: [ { tag: string, payload?: BeadSchema } ] }
  | Union   { options: [ BeadSchema ] }
  | Array   { element: BeadSchema }
  | Set     { element: BeadSchema, on?: [ LocalName ] }
  | TypeRef { to: TypeRef }                                  // TYPES-PASS: was `to: Kind`
  | JsonSchema { text: string }

## LocalName
/// description: A short identifier token for a view or a well-known name; leading letter.
/// jsonschema = { pattern = "^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$" }
type LocalName is string

// --- element references (how one overlay element names another) ---

## PackageElementRef
/// description: A bundled reference to a package element — a package authority plus the element's relative path.
/// normative: `authority` is OPTIONAL — a same-package (relative) reference omits it, as `refines` does within a package. A resource's own type is this pair unbundled into its `authority` + `type` envelope fields; PackageElementRef is the standalone / cross-package form.
type PackageElementRef {
  /// description: The package that names the element; absent ⇒ relative to the current package.
  authority?: PackageRef
  /// description: The element's package-relative path.
  path:       ElementPath
}

## ElementPath
/// description: A package-relative element path (the single-`is` chain).
type ElementPath = PathString


// Open: could `TypeRef` / `OperationRef` / `ChannelRef` collapse into one `packref<T>`?
## TypeRef
/// description: A reference to a type element.
type TypeRef      = PackageElementRef
## OperationRef
/// description: A reference to an operation element.
type OperationRef = PackageElementRef
## ChannelRef
/// description: A reference to a channel element.
type ChannelRef   = PackageElementRef

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2.2  Channels & the streaming model — pull, push
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A channel carries a sequence of events; `pull` reads it and `push` writes it. A
// read of a sequence is one of two natures: a LIVE feed (`pull` a channel) is a
// `source<T>`; a FINITE cut (a scope listing, a resource's history, an export) is a
// `snapshot<T>`. BDP offers one streaming convention over both; the nature decides
// which transport forms are valid (see the streaming model below).


## Channel
/// description: A channel-descriptor bead body — its two event schemas, direction, and delivery characteristics. Its resource is `rep<Channel>`.
/// normative: `direction` and the two schemas are mutually constraining — `source` ⟺ pullSchema set & pushSchema null; `sink` ⟺ pushSchema set & pullSchema null; `duplex` ⟺ both set.
/// normative: A Channel bead's revision does NOT change when events arrive — the bead is not the stream. Appended events ride the channel's own stream, never the changefeed.
type Channel is Bead {
  /// description: What `push` accepts; null on a source (pull-only).
  pushSchema?: BeadSchema
  /// description: What `pull` delivers; null on a sink (push-only).
  pullSchema?: BeadSchema
  /// description: The advertised facet — source · sink · duplex.
  direction:   "source" | "sink" | "duplex"
  /// description: How far back a cursor can resume.
  retention:   Retention
  /// description: Delivery cardinality — single or broadcast.
  cardinality: "single" | "broadcast"
}

## Retention
/// description: How far back a cursor can resume; below the floor a cursor faults `cursor-expired` and the reader resyncs (list-bootstrap + follow from head).
type Retention = union {
  /// description: Every event is resumable; the stream is never trimmed.
  Forever
  /// description: Resumable within a trailing window, bounded by time, count, or size.
  Window    { by: "time" | "count" | "size", amount: integer }
  /// description: Compacted to latest-per-key; an optional window bounds the compacted tail.
  Compacted { window?: Retention }
}

// --- the streaming model: two natures, three encodings, cursors, windows ---
// A read of an ordered, resumable sequence of `T` has one of two NATURES, and the
// nature — not the reader — decides which transport encodings are valid:
//
//   • `source<T>`   = a LIVE feed: a changefeed or declared stream, `pull`ed from a
//                     Channel. It has a live edge that keeps growing.
//   • `snapshot<T>` = a FINITE cut: `list` (a scope's current resources), `revisions`
//                     (one resource's history), `export` (a scope backup), and the
//                     stdlib `query`. Taken as of a cursor; nothing follows it.
//
// A reader consumes either in one of three transport encodings — each a typed
// projection the nature declares support for (the `--format` / Accept arm):
//
//   | encoding | type       | wire               | valid on          | notes                        |
//   | windowed | page<T>     | application/json   | source · snapshot | one request → one Page<T>    |
//   | bulk     | jsonl<T>    | application/jsonl  | source · snapshot | the whole sequence, T/line   |
//   | live     | sse<T>      | text/event-stream  | source only       | follow the feed as it grows  |
//
// `page<T>` and `jsonl<T>` are PULL/drainable, so they fit either nature. `sse<T>` is
// the only PUSH encoding — it follows a live edge — so it is LIVE-ONLY: a `source` may
// select it; a `snapshot` has nothing to follow and the encoding is not in its support
// set (so the invalid cell is unrepresentable in the type, not merely a runtime fault;
// the `400` survives only as the raw-wire guard for a client that hand-forges `?format=sse`).
// `page<T>` is NOT a new type — it is the existing `Page` window ([[core.lumen#Page]])
// parameterized on `T` (shorthand for `Page<T>`).
//
// One Cursor spans all three encodings of a `source`: page in json, catch up in jsonl,
// hand the same cursor to sse to go live.
// The transport enum is `Transport`, the response window is `Page`, and the request
// bundle is `ReadRequest` — ONE request type, shared by every read; its valid `format`
// set is DERIVED from the nature (a `source` admits `sse`, a `snapshot` does not).
// RESOLVED (return unified): EVERY read — `pull` (source) / `list` · `revisions` · the
// stdlib `query` · `export` (snapshot) — wire-returns a `Page` window `{ items, cursor,
// head? }`. The nature is the logical sequence; `Page` is its one-request window, the
// universal read envelope, so `list`/`query` return a Page, NOT a bare array. `cursor`
// resumes AFTER its last item (exclusive, opaque; null/absent once `head`). With `limit`
// absent a read returns the whole current set in one Page, BOUNDED by the service's
// advertised `maxLimit` — a larger set returns `maxLimit` items + a resume `cursor`, never
// unbounded. One Cursor spans the page / jsonl / sse transports.

## Ordinal
/// description: An event's position in its stream; monotone within one Channel.
type Ordinal is integer

## Cursor
/// description: Opaque, transport-independent resume token, bound to one stream and the selection it came from; refused elsewhere. Also the point an export is cut at.
type Cursor is string

## Transport
/// description: The transport encoding a caller selects (the `--format` arm): `page` (windowed JSON pages), `jsonl` (bulk JSON-lines), or `sse` (live SSE). The token mirrors the typed projection — `page<T>` / `jsonl<T>` / `sse<T>`. `page`/`jsonl` are valid on either nature; `sse` is valid only on a `source` (a live feed, `pull`), and a `snapshot` (list/query/revisions/export) does not carry it in its support set.
/// normative: `sse` transport is valid ONLY on a live source (pull); a snapshot read (list/query/revisions/export) refuses `format=sse` and faults `400`.
type Transport = "page" | "jsonl" | "sse"

## ReadRequest
/// description: The input to a streaming read — where to resume, how much, the transport, and per-item shaping. ONE request type for every read, `source` ([[core.lumen#pull]]) and `snapshot` ([[core.lumen#list]] / [[core.lumen#revisions]] / [[core.lumen#export]] / stdlib `query`) alike; the valid `format` set is DERIVED from the read's nature (a `source` admits `sse`, a `snapshot` does not). `after` (a cursor) xor `from` (a fresh ordinal) are mutually exclusive. The target is the op's first parameter, not restated here.
/// normative: Supplying both `after` and `from` is malformed and faults `400`.
/// normative: A service offering a read MUST HONOR the floor controls it accepts — not merely declare them: `after` (resume from the cursor), `limit` (window the result), and `format` (encode in the selected transport) — and MUST fault `400` on a malformed value. Silently ignoring a resume cursor or a transport selector — returning the whole set from the start, or the default encoding, as if the param were absent — is NON-CONFORMANT (the silent-failure class, like a dropped `where`). A declared floor param is a promise to act on it.
type ReadRequest = {
  /// description: Resume at an opaque cursor (exact).
  after?:  Cursor,
  /// description: Start at a known ordinal (xor `after`).
  from?:   Ordinal,
  /// description: Cap the window size.
  limit?:  integer,
  /// description: The transport encoding (`page`/`jsonl`/`sse`); `sse` only on a `source`.
  format?: Transport,
  /// description: Filter the stream.
  filter?: Filter,
  /// description: Project each event.
  select?: selector
}

## Page
/// description: One finite page of a stream — the windowed floor every service offers: one request, one page, nothing held open.
type Page = {
  /// description: The items in this window (a Channel's E — ChangeEvent for the changefeed; items generally).
  items:  [ any ],
  /// description: Where to resume; opaque, bound to this stream + selection.
  cursor: Cursor,
  /// description: Caught up to the live edge?
  head?:  bool
}

## pull
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "pull" }
/// description: Read a Channel — the changefeed (well-known `/sys/changes`) or a declared stream — as a `source<T>` (a LIVE feed) of its `pullSchema` events. `--format` picks the transport (`page` windowed / `jsonl` bulk / `sse` live); one Cursor spans all three. `sse` is valid here because `pull` is the one live-feed read.
/// normative: Valid on a `source`/`duplex` channel; a `sink`-only channel faults `channel-not-pullable`. Faults `cursor-expired` below the retention floor.
/// openapi = { operationId = "pull", method = "GET", pathItem = "channel", tags = ["Core"], params = { after = { in = "query", schema = "Cursor", required = false }, from = { in = "query", schema = "Ordinal", required = false }, limit = { in = "query", schema = "number", required = false }, format = { in = "header", schema = "Transport", required = false } }, success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [400,405,410] } }
fn pull(
    /// description: The channel to read — the changefeed or a declared stream.
    channel: unpinned<Channel>,
    /// description: Where to resume, how much, and the transport (see [[core.lumen#ReadRequest]]).
    request: ReadRequest
  ) -> Page

## push
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "push" }
/// description: Append one or more events to a Channel. The pusher offers a batch and the channel accepts what it can take now — `push` is non-blocking: it accepts a prefix, defers the rest under backpressure, and the pusher re-offers from `acceptedThrough + 1`. The pusher owns the pump. A keyed event (carrying a pusher-assigned `ordinal`) is idempotent — a re-push is a benign no-op. `jsonl` body (bulk).
/// normative: Valid on a `sink`/`duplex` channel; a `source`-only channel faults `channel-not-pushable`. A terminal (closed/failed) channel faults; a `single`-cardinality channel caps the batch at one.
/// normative: Append is idempotent on `(channel, ordinal)`. A keyed event — one carrying a pusher-assigned `ordinal` — is first-write-wins and immutable: a re-push of that ordinal is a benign no-op success, reported `Deduped`, never a fault. A single logical writer per channel makes pusher-assigned ordinals collision-free. An event with no `ordinal` is appended and the channel assigns one, reported `Accepted`.
/// normative: Backpressure rides the acknowledgement, not a reverse stream. The channel accepts a prefix of the batch up to its current admission `credit` (advertised as `window` in discovery), marks the remainder `Deferred`, and returns `acceptedThrough` (the high-water ordinal) with `credit` (how many more it will take now) and an optional `retryAfter`. The pusher holds the deferred tail and re-offers from `acceptedThrough + 1` after `retryAfter`; because re-offer is idempotent, the pusher tracks no per-event acknowledgement. The channel initiates nothing — it only annotates each pusher-initiated call.
/// normative: Backpressure — a `PushAck` carrying `credit: 0` and a deferred tail — is soft and temporal, not an error. It is distinct from `limit-exceeded` (422), the hard rejection of a request that exceeds a static capability bound (e.g. `maxBatch`), fixed by shrinking the request.
/// openapi = { operationId = "push", method = "POST", pathItem = "channel", tags = ["Core"], request = { contentType = "application/jsonl" }, success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [405,409,422] } }
command push(
    /// description: The channel to append to — must accept writes (`sink`/`duplex`).
    channel: unpinned<Channel>,
    /// description: One or more events to append, in order; each the channel's `pushSchema` type. A keyed event carries a pusher-assigned `ordinal`.
    events:  [PushEvent]
  ) -> PushAck
// Single-writer assumption (v0.2): a channel has one logical writer, so
// pusher-assigned ordinals are collision-free and `(channel, ordinal)` idempotency
// needs no strategy. A multi-writer channel (ordinals not collision-free) would
// reintroduce a fault-vs-skip push strategy — an additive, non-breaking extension,
// out of scope for v0.2.

## PushEvent
/// description: One event offered to [[core.lumen#push]]. `body` is the payload (the channel's `pushSchema` type); `ordinal`, when present, is the pusher-assigned position that makes the append idempotent (keyed). Absent, the channel assigns the ordinal.
type PushEvent = {
  /// description: The pusher-assigned position, for a keyed (idempotent) append; absent, the channel assigns one.
  ordinal?: Ordinal,
  /// description: The event payload — the channel's `pushSchema` type.
  body:     { ...: any }
}

## PushAck
/// description: What [[core.lumen#push]] returns — the high-water ordinal accepted, a per-event result, and the backpressure signal (`credit` + optional `retryAfter`). A short `results` with `credit: 0` and a deferred tail is normal flow, not an error.
type PushAck = {
  /// description: The high-water ordinal durably appended by this call; the pusher re-offers from `acceptedThrough + 1`.
  acceptedThrough: Ordinal,
  /// description: Per offered event, in order — appended, deduped (idempotent re-push), or deferred under backpressure.
  results:         [PushResult],
  /// description: How many more events the channel will accept right now (admission credit); `0` means hold and retry.
  credit:          integer,
  /// description: How long to wait before re-offering the deferred tail (seconds; maps to HTTP `Retry-After`).
  retryAfter?:     integer
}

## PushResult
/// description: The fate of one offered event.
type PushResult = union {
  /// description: Appended at this ordinal.
  Accepted { ordinal: Ordinal }
  /// description: Already present at this ordinal (idempotent re-push) — a benign no-op.
  Deduped  { ordinal: Ordinal }
  /// description: Not accepted on this call (backpressure); re-offer after `retryAfter`.
  Deferred
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2.3  Operations — call, cancel
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// An operation is a declared callable. `call` invokes it (sync or async); it is the
// only primitive with the rich Outcome (a user Operation has a declared
// success/failure in its signature; the data primitives do not).

## Operation
/// description: An operation-descriptor bead body — the call contract, the implementation, and the behavioural guarantees. Its resource is `rep<Operation>`.
/// normative: The effect markers (`pure`/`safe`/`idempotent`/`atomic`) are three-valued — present = a known guarantee, absent = unknown. `pure` ⟹ `safe`; `pure:true` + `safe:false` is a rejectable contradiction. The Lumen surface callable keywords lower to these fields (`fn` ⇒ pure).
type Operation is Bead {
  /// description: The call contract.
  signature:       Signature
  /// description: The mounted AST. Present ⇒ stored (ships in the pack) · absent ⇒ intrinsic (native).
  implementation?: any
  /// description: Known to neither read nor write external state.
  pure?:           bool
  /// description: Known not to write (reads allowed); pure ⟹ safe.
  safe?:           bool
  /// description: Known to be idempotent.
  idempotent?:     bool
  /// description: Known to commit as a single transaction. deferred to the scheduler.
  atomic?:         bool
}

// Open: define `fn` / `command` at the core level, or leave it to the upper layers (packs induce it)?

## Signature
/// description: An operation's call contract — its interface, kept separate from the implementation and the effect markers.
type Signature {
  /// description: The input record — the operation's parameter declarations.
  parameters?: [ ParameterDeclaration ]
  /// description: The success type (T).
  success?:    BeadSchema
  /// description: The declared failure type (E).
  failure?:    BeadSchema
  /// description: Async disposition — sync-only · caller-picks · async-only.
  async?:      "no" | "optional" | "required"
  /// description: Whether a successful call mints a resource → 201 + Location.
  creates?:    bool
}

// --- the call protocol: correlation, request/reply, result/outcome/problem ---

## Cause
/// description: A correlation token shared down a causal chain — minted at an initiating (often detached) call and propagated unchanged to every call it sets off; `causedBy` points at the parent cause. Correlates all of one initiation's work. See bdp.md §6.
type Cause       is string
## CallId
/// description: A correlation token naming one call — a node in the call tree; `calledBy` points at the parent call. Distinct from `Cause` (correlation to an initiation) and `Transaction` (the atomic commit). Diagnostic-tier.
type CallId      is string
## Transaction
/// description: A correlation token naming one atomic commit — shared by every change that commits together. Propagated by enlistment, not unconditionally (Required / RequiresNew / detach); crossing a scope forces a new one. See bdp.md §6.
type Transaction is string
## IdempotencyKey
/// description: Caller-chosen key that makes a retried write safe to collapse onto the first effect.
/// jsonschema = { pattern = "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,256}$" }
type IdempotencyKey is string

## Request
/// description: What a caller sends to `call`. `arguments` is a name/value list whose shape the Operation's `parameters` dictate.
type Request = {
  /// description: The positional-by-name arguments, shaped by the Operation's `parameters`.
  arguments:      [ { name: string, value: any } ],
  /// description: Request async disposition (default sync).
  async:          bool = false,
  /// description: The cause that triggered this call (correlation).
  causedBy?:      Cause,
  /// description: The parent call — its CallId (correlation, diagnostic-tier).
  calledBy?:      CallId,
  /// description: The caller's part of provenance only (principals/message/metadata); never server fields.
  provenance?:    Provenance,
  /// description: Collapses a retried call onto the first effect.
  idempotencyKey: IdempotencyKey,
  /// description: Opaque per-call sidecar.
  // Open: rename the per-call metadata fields to requestMetadata / responseMetadata (and provenanceMetadata)?
  metadata?:      Metadata
}

## Reply
/// description: What a `call` returns — flat, no context wrapper. `outcome` keeps the domain `Result` nested in `Outcome`.
/// normative: The `outcome`'s `T` and `E` are those declared by the target Operation's signature (`success` / `failure`); the envelope itself carries `Outcome<any, any>` and is narrowed only at the call site.
type Reply = {
  /// description: What happened — the domain Result or a non-domain fate.
  outcome:      Outcome<any, any>,
  /// description: The cause this call ran under (correlation).
  cause:        Cause,
  /// description: This call's id (correlation).
  callId:       CallId,
  /// description: The commit this call belonged to, if any.
  transaction?: Transaction,
  /// description: Opaque per-reply sidecar.
  metadata?:    Metadata
}

## Result
/// description: A callable's own verdict — succeeded with a value, or failed with its declared error.
type Result<T, E> = union {
  /// description: Succeeded, carrying the value.
  success { value: T }
  /// description: Failed with the declared error (E).
  failure { error: E }
}

## Outcome
/// description: What happened to an invocation — the domain Result, or a non-domain fate.
/// normative: The non-domain set (`fault` · `canceled`) is open — a reader MUST tolerate an unrecognized arm. This is the one sanctioned open union: the evolution surface for invocation fates (a closed union cannot gain arms). A domain failure is `Result.failure`; a non-domain failure is `fault`.
/// normative: A present `outcome` means the invocation is settled, whichever arm it holds; a transient state (running/waiting) is NOT an arm. Each arm is an open record — a reader MUST ignore a member it does not know. Only `success` means the operation went as intended.
/// normative: Wire encoding — `Result` and `Outcome` lower to an INTERNALLY-TAGGED JSON object with a `kind` discriminator: `{ "kind": "success", "value": T }` · `{ "kind": "failure", "error": E }` · `{ "kind": "fault", "problem": Problem }` · `{ "kind": "canceled", "reason"?: string }`. The HTTP status aligns (200 success · 409 the domain failure · the Problem's own status for a fault · 202 for an async `AsyncResult`), but `kind` is explicit in the body so a reader never infers the arm from the status alone.
type Outcome<T, E> = union {
  /// description: The domain Result — success | failure.
  Result<T, E>
  /// description: An undeclared failure (NOT an E) — carries a Problem.
  fault { problem: Problem }
  /// description: Kill -9 — runs no compensation. `timed_out` is a cancel reason, with the scheduler.
  canceled { reason?: string }
}
// >DB? you rejected an explicit `other { kind }` arm (it just moves the problem down
// a level). Left as a declared-open union instead — new fates ride as new arms that
// old readers tolerate. Confirm this is the resolution you want.

## Problem
/// description: An RFC 9457 problem document — what a `fault` carries, and the body of a failing response from the data primitives.
/// normative: `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail` and `instance` are the RFC 9457 standard members; `code` and `retry` are BDP EXTENSION members (RFC 9457 permits extension members). `type` is a stable, machine-readable problem-type URI identifier (`tag:bdp.dev,2026:problem/<code>`, one per `code` — Appendix D.9), not a bare token. A `tag:` URI (RFC 4151) identifies without being dereferenceable, which RFC 9457 §3.1.1 permits.
/// normative: The fault model. Every primitive returns its declared value on success or faults with a Problem — delivered out of band as the HTTP non-2xx response (RFC 9457), never part of the declared return type; a fault is orthogonal to the value. Only `call` additionally carries a declared failure (`Outcome.failure`, HTTP 409) — an operation's own "no". The data primitives have none: a read returns a `rep` or faults. A Problem is the universal fault channel, not the `E` of a `Result`.
type Problem = {
  /// description: A URI naming the problem kind.
  type:      AbsoluteUri,
  /// description: A short human summary.
  title:     string,
  /// description: The HTTP status code.
  status:    integer,
  /// description: A stable, machine-readable problem code.
  code:      string,
  /// description: Whether a retry can help.
  retry:     "never" | "after-state-change" | "after-delay",
  /// description: An optional human detail.
  detail?:   string,
  /// description: An optional URI for the specific occurrence.
  instance?: AbsoluteUri,
  /// description: RFC 9457 §3.2 extension members. `code` and `retry` are BDP's own; a service MAY add others, so a Problem document is open. Closing it would forbid the extensibility this type's own normative note relies on.
  ...: any
}

## AsyncResult
/// description: The async result of one `call` — a handle you show for the record and `cancel` to kill. References a one-and-done source Channel that fires the Reply on settlement.
type AsyncResult is Bead {
  /// description: The operation that was called.
  operation:    ref<Operation>,
  /// description: The cause this call ran under (queryable while pending, before `reply`).
  cause:        Cause,
  /// description: This call's id (queryable while pending).
  callId:       CallId,
  /// description: The original request.
  request:      Request,
  /// description: The settlement, once it arrives (absent while pending).
  reply?:       Reply,
  /// description: The one-and-done source Channel that fires the Reply on settlement.
  replyChannel: ref<Channel>
}
// Note (resolved): as a bead (below) this inherits provenance/createdAt/metadata from the
// envelope. `cause`/`callId` are kept at top-level (queryable while `reply` is absent),
// per your "would you query it while pending?" note.

## call
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "call" }
/// description: Invoke an Operation. Sync (default) returns the Outcome directly (200 / 201+Location when `creates` / 409 on a declared failure / Problem status on `fault`). `?mode=async` returns 202 + the AsyncResult at Location; settle later. The only primitive with the rich Outcome.
/// normative: Dispatch rule — `call` is a primitive and may fault with a `Problem` like any primitive. A PRE-dispatch failure (unresolvable `operation`, malformed `request`, unauthorized, idempotency-key mismatch) faults as a `Problem` with NO `Outcome`: the operation never ran and nothing is recorded. A POST-dispatch failure rides the `Outcome` (`Result.failure` → 409; `fault { problem }` → the Problem's status; `canceled`). So a bare `Problem` means the call could not run; a `Problem` inside `Outcome.fault` means the operation ran and faulted — the recorded `AsyncResult.reply` is the witness that it dispatched.
/// normative: Addressing — an INTRINSIC operation (implementation absent, [[core.lumen#Operation]]) is addressed by its well-known element path (e.g. `issue/claim`); `operation: ref<Operation>` resolves to that path, and the client does NOT materialize an Operation bead first. A stored operation (implementation present) is an ordinary resolvable bead.
/// openapi = { operationId = "call", method = "POST", pathItem = "operation", tags = ["Core"], params = { mode = { in = "query", schema = "\"sync\" | \"async\"", required = false } }, responses = [ { status = 200, schema = "Reply", description = "sync ok" }, { status = 201, schema = "Reply", description = "sync ok, mints a resource" }, { status = 409, schema = "Reply", description = "declared failure — branch on the arm" }, { status = 202, schema = "rep<AsyncResult>", description = "async — result at Location" } ], responseHeaders = { Location = { schema = "AbsoluteHttpUrl" } } }
fn call(
    /// description: The operation to invoke (see [[core.lumen#Operation]]).
    operation: ref<Operation>,
    /// description: The call input — arguments, idempotency key, and cause (see [[core.lumen#Request]]).
    request:   Request
  ) -> Reply

## cancel
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "cancel" }
/// description: Kill -9 on a running AsyncResult — writes `reply.outcome = canceled`. Runs no compensation (that is the scheduler's graceful path); its effects stay findable via `causedBy`.
/// normative: `cancel` settles an async result as `canceled` and runs NO compensation; effects already produced stay findable via `causedBy`.
/// openapi = { operationId = "cancel", method = "POST", pathItem = "asyncResult", tags = ["Core"], success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [404,409] } }
command cancel(
    /// description: The running async handle to stop (see [[core.lumen#AsyncResult]]).
    asyncResult: unpinned<AsyncResult>
  ) -> pinned<AsyncResult>

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2.4  Packages
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A package is the authority that names a bead's type — a versioned bundle of
// overlay elements (type / operation / channel defs) and file assets.

## PackageDef
/// description: A package bead body — its declared version and its elements. Its resource is `rep<PackageDef>`.
/// normative: A package is a resource; its canonical identity is its own `id` ({ scope:"uri", id:<url>, rev:<pin> }) — exactly what beads stamp as their `authority`. It is NOT restated in the body.
type PackageDef is Bead {
  /// description: The version this package declares; refs pin to a resolved one via their `rev`.
  semVer:   SemVer
  /// description: Elements keyed by package-relative path; value = any resource — bead defs and file assets.
  elements: { ...: Resource }
}



// #############################################################################
// 3. Primitives — the uniform set
// #############################################################################
// Dispatch is implicit: a request routes to the target resource. Beads take client
// CRUD (create/put/patch/delete/list/revisions); the three model-specific
// primitives live with their models (§1.2 `get`, §2.2 `pull`/`push`, §2.3
// `call`/`cancel`). This section is the uniform write/read surface over any
// resource, then the export & change-notification cluster.
//
// --- the HTTP route table (relative to a scope's base URL) ---
// Each primitive's `openapi.pathItem` (resource / scope / channel / operation /
// asyncResult) resolves to a concrete route below. The URL space has three parts:
//   • an id space — a resource is `/{id}`, where {id} is a free, slash-bearing string;
//   • noun roots — `/sys/*` for in-band system RESOURCES (addressed and read with
//     `get`, e.g. `/sys/changes`, `/sys/capabilities`) and `/bdp/*` for the protocol's
//     own fixed routes & artifacts (`/bdp/openapi` — static artifacts only);
//   • the ACTION FENCE `/-/` — a bare `-` segment separates a resource id (left) from a
//     protocol action or projection (right). The fence (NOT a `:verb` suffix) is what
//     carries actions, because an id is a free string: a `:` has URL-reference scheme
//     ambiguity and the fleet's ids already bear colons (timestamp ids). At the scope
//     root the left side is empty, so `/-/list`, `/-/create` are scope actions.
//     The fence is parsed on the RAW path (pre-decode) as the LAST `/-/`; the right
//     side is validated against the closed, fence-free action set (else 404), and an
//     id with a literal `-`-only segment is percent-encoded (`%2D`) — a write minting
//     an unencoded bare `-` segment is refused — so no `%2D` forges the fence.
// So `GET /` is free to be the discovery signpost, and nothing collides with an id.
//
//   primitive   method  route                      notes
//   discovery   GET     /                          → ServiceDescriptor (the signpost; links mirrored in a Link header)
//   discovery   GET     /sys/capabilities          live, per-deployment Capabilities (a `get` on a /sys bead)
//   discovery   GET     /bdp/openapi               the static OpenAPI contract (RFC 8631 service-desc)
//   get         GET     /{id}                      ?include=/?omit=/?expand= (or ?select=) projection; ?revision= time-travel
//   list        GET     /-/list                    scope list → Page (moved off the bare root)
//   create      POST    /-/create                  body = the rep (body: any); fail-if-exists → identity-taken
//   put         PUT     /{id}                      If-Match (OCC) · If-None-Match:* (create-if-absent) · ?precondition= (state guard)
//   patch       PATCH   /{id}                      json-patch+json body · If-Match · ?precondition=
//   delete      DELETE  /{id}                      If-Match · ?precondition=
//   revisions   GET     /{id}/-/revisions          the resource's history → Page
//   call        POST    /{opPath}/-/call           +?mode=async → 202 + AsyncResult Location
//   cancel      POST    /{asyncResultId}/-/cancel  {asyncResultId} = the async Location
//   pull        GET     /{channel}                 a Channel, e.g. /sys/changes (changefeed)
//   push        POST    /{channel}                 append jsonl to a sink/duplex Channel
//   export      GET     /-/export                  bulk scope serialization → JSONL (scope action)
//   import      POST    /-/import                  bulk load ← JSONL → ImportAck (resumable; scope action)
//
// `push` is NOT the stdlib `batch`: it appends to a Channel. A read whose query string
// overruns the URL budget MAY re-send under the fence as a body-form POST (`/-/list`,
// `/{id}/-/get`) carrying the `ReadRequest`, and an over-budget write guard MAY spill to
// `POST /{id}/-/{put|patch|delete}` carrying an `Envelope` — body-form spill is OPTIONAL
// and advertised (a minimal scope rejects over-budget with `request-too-large`). See Appendix D.
//
// Discovery, not tiers (§3.3): a scope advertises WHICH primitives it offers, à la
// carte. The mandatory floor is `get`, the `GET /` ServiceDescriptor, and a readable
// `/sys/capabilities`; every other primitive is present iff advertised. A request for
// an un-offered primitive is refused `capability-not-supported`.
//
// Discovery ALSO serves the connected packs' overlay: the TYPE definitions (their
// property declarations + cli hints — cli.alias / cli.positional / cli.default /
// cli.edge) and the OPERATION signatures (parameters + each op's declared
// domain-error set), so a client renders the available typed commands from discovery
// alone, with no out-of-band pack knowledge.
//
// Shape — the capabilities document is { scope, base, repModel, defaultType?,
// capabilities (the primitives + call), packs[] }. (No top-level `operations` list —
// `packs[].operations` is authoritative; the flat bare-path back-compat list is dropped.)
// `defaultType` is the scope's deployment default concrete
// type for a bare `create` (a per-scope RUNTIME fact, NOT a pack hint); absent ⇒ a bare
// create with no --type faults, listing the creatable types.
// Each pack is { name, version?, cli?{ mount }, types[], operations[] }:
//   type = { type (kind-path), bead, abstract, extends?, summary?, properties[
//            { name, type, required, computed?, cli?{ alias, flag, positional, default } } ],
//            (`computed: true` marks a derived read-only property, so a client can omit
//             EMPTY computed collections by default without guessing — §5 shaping)
//            edgeFlags[ { flag, summary?, op, self, other, args? } ] (type-level cli.edge,
//            lowered — one `create` flag that also invokes an edge op; named `edgeFlags`
//            to disambiguate from a `dep edges` op; see bdp-cli.md) }
//   op   = { path (call-by-path), kind ("command" | "fn"), summary?, parameters[
//            { name, type, required, cli?{ … } } ], errors[] (the Result E set), returns }
// `cli.alias` is the bare short letter ("s" → the CLI renders -s); `cli.positional` is
// a 0-based slot; `cli.default` is a JSON value; `cli.flag` overrides the long flag name
// (presentational only — e.g. `estimatedMinutes` renders `--estimate`; the property name
// is unchanged). An op `path` splits on "/" into nested
// subcommands (`issue/dep/add` → `bdp issue dep add`). The generic CRUD verbs
// (create/show/list/update/delete/get) are the UNIVERSAL primitives the CLI knows
// intrinsically — it parameterises `create <type>` from the type's properties + cli
// hints; the catalog's `operations` are the pack callables, rendered as typed verbs
// (not duplicated per-type). An abstract type (e.g. `issue`) is not directly
// instantiable: with no explicit `--type`, `create` takes the scope's
// advertised `defaultType` (the deployment default; a client `$BDP_DEFAULT_TYPE` may
// override), else it lists the subtypes. A pack MUST NOT declare a default type — there
// is no `cli.defaultType` catalog hint; the default is a scope/client fact ONLY, and the
// feature is discouraged (prefer an explicit `--type`).
//
// Command placement — a pack sets `cli.mount` (where its commands attach to the
// aggregate `bd` tree: "/" = top-level so `bd ready`, a prefix like "issue" → `bd
// issue …`; default the pack name), and an operation sets `cli.name` (its command
// name, overriding the path-derived default; may contain spaces to nest, e.g.
// `issue/dep/add` → "dep add"). So `issue/ready` with `cli.name:"ready"` under a
// top-level pack renders `bd ready`, while the call-by-path (`bdp call issue/ready`)
// always works. `cli.mount` is the SINGLE placement choice — "/" renders the pack's
// commands flat with NO namespaced form, a prefix renders them only under that prefix;
// a pack does not get both. The call-by-path (`bdp call issue/ready`) is the invariant
// long form — for scripting, and for disambiguating a `cli.name` collision between two
// "/"-mounted packs. `--help` is rendered from the catalog (summaries, parameters,
// defaults, error sets).
//
// Static catalog — the pack overlay MAY be checked in and rendered offline. Its
// canonical form is the `packs` member VERBATIM: `{ packs: [ … ] }` (well-known
// `catalog.json`), byte-identical to `/sys/capabilities`.`packs`, so a client slices
// `.packs` off a live doc to produce one and renders typed commands from either
// source by the same path. ONLY the pack overlay is static — it is the versioned,
// deployment-independent part (type defs + op signatures + cli hints), and each pack
// MAY carry its `version` (SemVer) so a checked-in catalog pins exact pack versions
// for reproducible build-time codegen. The scope-level fields (scope / base /
// repModel / capabilities) are per-deployment RUNTIME facts a live endpoint supplies
// and are NOT part of the static catalog: rendering offline, a client assumes the
// full primitive floor, and a live `capabilities` later narrows which generic verbs
// apply. From this the CLI bootstraps — or build-time codegens typed, statically-
// checked commands — when it cannot reach a live endpoint.
//
// Error model: a data primitive returns its success value and faults with a Problem.
// Writes return a pinned ref; reads a rep; `delete` a tombstone Rev. (Ruling §0.8.)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3.1  Write & read the resource store — create, put, patch, delete, list, revisions
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## IdRoot
/// description: A prefix-rooted auto-id proposal (the server mints the leaf).
type IdRoot {
  /// description: The id prefix to mint under; the server appends the leaf segment.
  prefix: string
}
## Id
/// description: An exact caller-proposed id.
type Id {
  /// description: The exact id the caller proposes.
  id: string
}

## create
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "create" }
/// description: Admit a new Bead at `POST /-/create` (under the action fence; the bare root `GET /` is the ServiceDescriptor). Caller-proposed id optional; fail-if-exists → `identity-taken` (409). Returns the created resource's pinned `ref` (rev = the minted revision); its URL in `Location`. (Create-if-absent at a known id is `put` with `ifAbsent` / `If-None-Match: *`, not `create`.)
/// normative: `create` input is closed — an envelope member Core does not define is refused, not ignored (this does not reach inside `body`/`metadata`, which Core does not interpret). An id already in use, used and retired, or — when `aliases` is advertised — held by a live alias ([[core.lumen#Alias]]), is refused with `identity-taken`; an unrequested server-minted id likewise avoids live alias names.
/// openapi = { operationId = "create", method = "POST", pathItem = "scope", tags = ["Core"], success = { status = 201 }, responseHeaders = { Location = { schema = "AbsoluteHttpUrl" } }, problem = { status = [400,409,422] } }
command create(
    /// description: The scope to create the resource in.
    // Open: adopt `self` parameters here for the Lumen language bindings?
    scope:        Scope,
    /// description: Caller-proposed identity (no rev yet) — an exact `Id` or a prefix `IdRoot` the server mints the leaf under. Absent, the server assigns the id.
    requestedId?: IdRoot | Id,
    /// description: The bead's type path (e.g. `issue/task`).
    type:         PathString,
    /// description: The package that names `type`. Caller may supply; absent, the server resolves it from `type`.
    authority?:   PackageRef,
    /// description: The bead's body — the domain payload (`any` at core; the typed `body: T` is the pack-level view). The CLI lowers property flags into this record.
    body:         any,
    /// description: Optional open sidecar the protocol does not interpret.
    metadata?:    Metadata
  ) -> pinned<any>
// >DB? can `create` admit a content resource (media-type `type` + registry authority), or only `put`?

## put
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "put" }
/// description: Upsert / remember — idempotent full-state write. Create-or-replace; the lifecycle effect (create vs update) is what the changefeed reports. Returns the written pinned `ref` (rev = the new revision).
/// normative: `put` replaces resource state wholesale (a full-state write), NOT a merge.
/// normative: A full-state write that changes nothing succeeds, keeps the revision, records no provenance, and produces no event; the reply carries no `transaction`.
/// normative: WRITE GUARDS ARE PRECONDITIONS, NEVER BODY. A guard rides the request line (a header or the query string), never the payload — so a `put` body stays exactly the resource representation and a `patch` body stays exactly the JSON Patch, unwrapped and correctly typed. Three guards, carried by `put`, `patch` and `delete` (`put` also carries the second): (1) `ifRevision` → `If-Match: "<Rev>"` (the `Rev` as a double-quoted strong entity-tag, RFC 9110 §8.8.3), the optimistic-concurrency / revision guard — a stale value makes no change and faults `revision-mismatch` (412); (2) `ifAbsent` → `If-None-Match: *`, create-if-absent — the write applies only if nothing is there, else faults `identity-taken` (409 — the same status as a `create` collision, so a client has one id-taken handler) (`put` only); (3) `precondition` → `?precondition=<Filter>`, a STATE guard — the write applies only if the current resource satisfies the Filter, else faults `precondition-failed` (412). The state guard is the same flat-AND [[core.lumen#Filter]] grammar `list` uses, projected onto the query string exactly like a read filter. A guard too large for the URL budget MAY spill to an OPTIONAL, advertised `query`-tier body-form (`POST /{id}/-/{put|patch|delete}`, media type `application/vnd.bdp.envelope+json`) carrying an [[core.lumen#Envelope]] — `{ precondition?, representation }` for put (the FULL representation, never a bare body), `{ precondition?, patch }` for patch, `{ precondition? }` for delete — the one place an envelope appears; the `If-Match`/`If-None-Match` guards keep riding their headers, and a scope that does not advertise spill rejects an over-budget guard with `request-too-large`. See Appendix D. There is no revision-keyed read cursor: streaming reads resume by `after` (Cursor) xor `from` (Ordinal); a specific revision is read by `get` under `?revision=`.
/// openapi = { operationId = "put", method = "PUT", pathItem = "resource", tags = ["Core"], params = { ifRevision = { in = "header", name = "If-Match", schema = "Rev", required = false }, ifAbsent = { in = "header", name = "If-None-Match", schema = "\"*\"", required = false }, precondition = { in = "query", name = "precondition", schema = "Filter", required = false } }, success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [400,409,410,412,422] } }
idempotent command put(
    /// description: The resource to write (its id; `rev` ignored — `put` targets the current resource).
    resource:    unpinned<any>,
    /// description: The bead's type path.
    type:        PathString,
    /// description: The package that names `type`. Caller may supply; absent, the server resolves it from `type`.
    authority?:  PackageRef,
    /// description: The full new body — `put` replaces state wholesale (`any` at core; typed `body: T` at the pack level). On the wire the request payload is the full representation (`type` + `authority?` + this `body` + `metadata?`); the guards below are request metadata, never part of it.
    body:        any,
    /// description: Optional open sidecar the protocol does not interpret.
    metadata?:   Metadata,
    /// description: Revision guard → `If-Match`. Apply only if this matches the current revision.
    ifRevision?: Rev,
    /// description: Create-if-absent guard → `If-None-Match: *`. Apply only if no resource exists at the id; else `identity-taken` (409).
    ifAbsent?:   bool,
    /// description: State guard → `?precondition=`. Apply only if the current resource satisfies this Filter; else `precondition-failed`. Rides the query string, not the body.
    precondition?: Filter
  ) -> pinned<any>
// Note: a separate id proposal was folded into the `resource` ref. The guards
// (ifRevision/ifAbsent/precondition) are request metadata — headers and the query
// string — so the body is only ever the resource. See the guard rule above.

## patch
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "patch" }
/// description: JSON Patch over the resource representation, ROOT-addressed (`/body/…`, `/metadata/…`, `/type`, `/authority`). The immutable envelope (`/id`, `/provenance`, `/createdAt`) and computed properties are read-only — a patch that touches one faults. Guards ride the request line (`If-Match` for `ifRevision`; `?precondition=` for a state guard), so the body stays exactly the JSON Patch. Returns the patched resource's pinned `ref`. (JSON Patch is the base format; a richer patch format is an advertised capability.)
/// normative: A patch whose path names an envelope member (`/id`, `/provenance`, `/createdAt`), the whole-document root (`""`), or a computed property faults — for ANY JSON Patch operation, and for the `from` pointer of `move`/`copy` — whatever the implementation's patch document contains.
/// openapi = { operationId = "patch", method = "PATCH", pathItem = "resource", tags = ["Core"], params = { ifRevision = { in = "header", name = "If-Match", schema = "Rev", required = false }, precondition = { in = "query", name = "precondition", schema = "Filter", required = false } }, request = { contentType = "application/json-patch+json" }, success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [404,409,410,412,415,422] } }
command patch(
    /// description: The resource to patch (its current revision).
    resource:    unpinned<any>,
    /// description: The JSON Patch to apply — root-addressed over the rep (`/body`, `/metadata`, `/type`, `/authority`; the rest is read-only). This IS the request payload (`application/json-patch+json`); the guards below are not part of it.


// Resolved: `type` / `authority` are mutated by patching `/type` / `/authority` at root — no distinct arguments. Root-addressing means a layout change never changes this signature.

    patch:       JsonPatch,
    /// description: Revision guard → `If-Match`. Apply only if this matches the current revision.
    ifRevision?: Rev,
    /// description: State guard → `?precondition=`. Apply only if the current resource satisfies this Filter; else `precondition-failed`. Rides the query string, not the body.
    precondition?: Filter
  ) -> pinned<any>

## delete
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "delete" }
/// description: Retire a Resource, minting a final (tombstone) revision. Idempotent. Inbound references are owned by other beads' revisions, so they may dangle; a service that enforces referential integrity faults when inbound references pin it (advertised, not base). Returns the tombstone Rev, not a representation.
/// normative: Wire body — a bare `Rev` return (`delete`, and any `-> Rev` callable such as `removeDependency`) lowers to a minimal self-describing object `{ "rev": <Rev> }`, NOT a raw JSON string; the `ETag` header mirrors it.
/// normative: Delete is not erasure — a deleted resource's id stays accounted for, and its tombstone revision is kept with the delete's provenance (naming the last live as `previousRevision`). Keeping that tombstone revision is revision-dependent, so it relaxes under `unversioned` ([[core.lumen#RevisionModel]]); the id accounting does not relax with it. Delete touches exactly one resource and does nothing else; it changes no identifier and no kind.
/// normative: Whether the retired id may be minted again is the scope's declared id model ([[core.lumen#IdModel]]), not a property of `delete`: under `permanent` the id is never reused, under `reusable` it may be. These are three separate obligations — id accounting, the tombstone revision, and id permanence — and only the second is revision-dependent.
/// normative: `delete` is idempotent on an EXISTING resource, not total. Deleting a resource that is already retired is the idempotent case: it returns the same tombstone `Rev` and does not fault, which is why `delete` alone among the writes declares no `410`. Deleting an id that NEVER existed is a different case and faults `404`, because an absent target is not a resource to retire: there is nothing for `delete` to act on, and the declared `404` has no other producer. Retiring an id is a consequence of retiring a resource, never a way to reserve or deny one — so `delete` MUST NOT mint a tombstone for an id it did not retire. That prohibition stands on the sentence before it and not on any implementation's behaviour; note only as a consequence of violating it that under `permanent` ([[core.lumen#IdModel]]) a minting `delete` would let any caller able to delete permanently spend any id in the space, since `create` at that id would then fault `identity-taken` for ever.
/// normative: `delete`'s idempotency is UNCONDITIONAL; only its `404`'s reachability varies, and it varies with whether the scope can tell the two cases apart. A `permanent` scope retains a record of every retirement (the retired-id accounting, [[core.lumen#IdModel]]) and MUST fault `404` for an id that never existed. A `reusable` scope retains no such record — and keeps no tombstone revision either, since `reusable` ⇒ `unversioned` ([[core.lumen#ScopeCapabilities]]) — so for it "already retired" and "never existed" are the same state: it answers the idempotent success, and its `404` is simply unreachable, exactly as a write's `410` is unreachable under `reusable`. Idempotency sits on the unconditional side because a fault that no scope can produce costs nothing, while a guarantee that varies by model forces every client to learn a scope's models before it can act. It is NOT justified by retry safety, and must not be read as making a `delete` retry safe.
/// normative: A write that TARGETS a retired resource faults `410 Gone`, so `put` and `patch` both declare it alongside `get`. It is distinct from its two neighbours: a never-retired missing id is `404`, and CLAIMING a spent id is `identity-taken` (409). On `put` the 409 is the **`ifAbsent` guard's** fault, never the operation's — every `put` is id-rooted (`PUT /{id}`), so rooting cannot discriminate and the guard is what does: a bare `put` targets the resource, so a retired target is `410`, while a `put` under `If-None-Match: *` asserts the id is free, so a spent id is `409` — the same status as a `create` collision, keeping one id-taken handler for the claiming path. Updating what is gone and claiming an identity that is spent are different faults, and on `put` the guard is what tells them apart. `put` declares no `404` because an absent id is not an error for it: `put` creates. Like `get`'s 410, a write's 410 is reachable only under `permanent`; under `reusable` a retired id is simply absent, so the write sees `404` or creates.
/// normative: Delete does not rewrite any other resource, so inbound references may dangle. A reference that pins a revision (`rev` present) still resolves — revisions are immutable, and a deleted resource's history is kept — so only an unpinned inbound reference dangles: resolving it (`get`) returns the current state, now the tombstone, and faults `410 Gone`. Enforcing referential integrity (faulting a delete whose target still has inbound pins) is an advertised capability, not base.
/// openapi = { operationId = "delete", method = "DELETE", pathItem = "resource", tags = ["Core"], params = { ifRevision = { in = "header", name = "If-Match", schema = "Rev", required = false }, precondition = { in = "query", name = "precondition", schema = "Filter", required = false } }, success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [404,409,412] } }
idempotent command delete(
    /// description: The resource to retire (its current revision).
    resource:    unpinned<any>,


// Todo: restore the cascade-delete flag (on delete) and a dry-run flag (on create / put / patch / delete).


    /// description: Revision guard → `If-Match`. Delete only if this matches the current revision.
    ifRevision?: Rev,
    /// description: State guard → `?precondition=`. Delete only if the current resource satisfies this Filter; else `precondition-failed`. Direct `DELETE` carries the guard on the request line; an over-budget delete guard MAY use the advertised POST spill (an `Envelope` with `precondition` only, no body).
    precondition?: Filter
  ) -> Rev
// >DB? delete → bare `Rev` (your instinct) breaks the "writes return a pinned ref"
// symmetry on purpose — a tombstone has no representation. Confirm.

// > **Open** — the CLIENT side of the recycled-id retry. A `reusable` scope must now
// > advertise a guard on `delete` ([[core.lumen#IdModel]]), so the mechanism is always
// > there; what is not yet stated is whether a client retrying a `delete` under `reusable`
// > MUST use it. Offering a guard does not oblige anyone to reach for it, and an unguarded
// > retry stays unsafe: A deletes X, the response is lost, B legitimately takes the freed
// > id, and A's retry destroys B's resource — reporting success, so A cannot learn it from
// > the result. `delete`'s idempotency makes the STATUS stable, never the effect. The
// > obligation would sit beside the reference rule in [[core.lumen#IdModel]]. Note that on a
// > `reusable` scope the guard is the ONLY such instrument: the parallel safety net for a
// > reference, a pinned revision, does not exist there, because `reusable` requires
// > `unversioned` ([[core.lumen#ScopeCapabilities]]). That makes the question sharper, not
// > softer — there is nothing else for a careful client to reach for.

## list
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "list" }
/// description: A collection of current resources in a scope, as a snapshot<Resource> (a FINITE cut). `where` is a Filter (the flat low bar every service supports); the full boolean/traversal `query` is a stdlib capability ([[stdlib.lumen#query]]). Snapshot — `--format page|jsonl`, no `sse`.
/// openapi = { operationId = "list", method = "GET", pathItem = "scope", tags = ["Core"], params = { where = { in = "query", schema = "Filter", required = false }, include = { in = "query", schema = "[JsonPointer]", required = false }, omit = { in = "query", schema = "[JsonPointer]", required = false }, expand = { in = "query", schema = "[LocalName]", required = false }, select = { in = "query", schema = "selector", required = false }, after = { in = "query", schema = "Cursor", required = false }, limit = { in = "query", schema = "number", required = false }, format = { in = "header", schema = "Transport", required = false } }, success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [400] } }
fn list(
    /// description: The scope to list.
    scope:   Scope,
    /// description: The low-bar filter — a flat conjunction of field conditions (overlaps `request.filter`; see the open shape note).


// Resolved: yes — put / patch / delete accept a state guard, carried as a `precondition`
// Filter on the request line (`?precondition=`), NOT in the body; it reuses this flat-AND
// `Filter` grammar and faults `precondition-failed`. See the guard rule on `put`.

    where?:  Filter,
    /// description: Projection applied to each result (overlaps `request.select`).
    select?: selector,
    /// description: Streaming controls — resume cursor, limit, transport (`page`/`jsonl`; no `sse`).
    request?: ReadRequest
  ) -> snapshot<Resource>

## revisions
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "revisions" }
/// description: The history of one resource — id-targeted like `get`, list-shaped like `list`. `select` controls payload (ids-only vs full representations). Snapshot.
/// openapi = { operationId = "revisions", method = "GET", pathItem = "resource", tags = ["Core"], params = { after = { in = "query", schema = "Cursor", required = false }, limit = { in = "query", schema = "number", required = false }, format = { in = "header", schema = "Transport", required = false } }, success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [400,404] } }
fn revisions(
    /// description: The resource whose history to read.
    resource: unpinned<any>,
    /// description: Projection per revision — ids-only vs full representations.
    select?:  selector,
    /// description: Streaming controls — resume cursor, limit, transport (`page`/`jsonl`; no `sse`).
    request?: ReadRequest
  ) -> snapshot<Resource>

// --- read-shaping & patch bodies (shared by the reads above) ---

## selector
/// description: A read projection over a representation — include/omit subtrees (JSON Pointer) and expand. Query-string, so a projected read has its own cacheable identity; `type`/`id` are never dropped.
/// normative: Query-string encoding — the three fields lower to the baseline params `?include=`, `?omit=` (comma-separated JSON Pointers) and `?expand=` (comma-separated names), matching the CLI `--include` / `--omit` / `--expand` flags; a leading `/` on a pointer is optional. `?select=<URL-encoded selector JSON>` is the complete form (the CLI `--select` shorthand), accepted for tooling and for a pointer that contains a literal comma. `type`/`id` are never dropped.
type selector {
  /// description: Keep only these subtrees (JSON Pointer); everything else is dropped.
  include?: [ JsonPointer ],
  /// description: Drop these subtrees; everything else is kept.
  omit?:    [ JsonPointer ],
  /// description: Expand these named references inline instead of leaving them as refs.
  expand?:  [ LocalName ]
}

## JsonPointer
/// description: A JSON Pointer (RFC 6901) into a representation.
type JsonPointer is string

## JsonPatchOp
/// description: One RFC 6902 operation. In `patch`, paths are root-addressed over the rep (`/body`, `/metadata`, `/type`, `/authority`; the rest is read-only).
type JsonPatchOp = {
  /// description: The RFC 6902 operation to perform.
  op:     "add" | "remove" | "replace" | "move" | "copy" | "test",
  /// description: The target location, as a JSON Pointer.
  path:   string,
  /// description: The value, for `add` / `replace` / `test`.
  value?: any,
  /// description: The source location, for `move` / `copy`.
  from?:  string
}

## JsonPatch
/// description: An ordered list of JSON Patch operations — the `patch` body, and a change-event patch payload.
type JsonPatch = [ JsonPatchOp ]

## Envelope
/// description: The query-tier body-form envelope — the ONE place a write guard rides a body. Used only when an over-budget `precondition` (or representation) spills to `POST /{id}/-/{put|patch|delete}`; the direct `put`/`patch`/`delete` verbs never carry it. Media type `application/vnd.bdp.envelope+json`.
/// normative: Support is OPTIONAL and advertised ([[core.lumen#ScopeCapabilities]] `bodyFormSpill`); a scope that does not offer it rejects an over-budget guard with `request-too-large`. Exactly one payload member by verb — `representation` for `put` (the FULL representation `{ type, authority?, body, metadata? }`, never a bare body), `patch` for `patch` (the raw RFC 6902 array carried as a JSON value governed by THIS envelope's media type, not `application/json-patch+json`), and none for `delete`. `precondition` is the optional state-guard Filter; the `If-Match` / `If-None-Match` guards keep riding their headers even in body-form.
type Envelope = {
  /// description: The state-guard Filter (the `?precondition=` guard, relocated to the body when over-budget).
  precondition?:   Filter,
  /// description: For `put` — the full representation to write (`{ type, authority?, body, metadata? }`).
  representation?: any,
  /// description: For `patch` — the RFC 6902 JSON Patch array, as a JSON value inside this envelope.
  patch?:          JsonPatch
}

## Filter
/// description: The low-bar list filter — a flat conjunction (and) of simple field conditions, exactly the query-string-expressible set. The baseline every service supports; the full boolean/traversal predicate is a stdlib capability (`query`, [[stdlib.lumen#query]]), not base.
type Filter = [ listCondition ]

## listCondition
/// description: One flat field condition. `isType` matches against the type-path (`type.id`): `prefix` ⊑ (the "is it an issue(-ish)?" test), `exact` =.
/// normative: Field paths are root-addressed `FieldPath` ARRAYS (`["body","status"]`, `["metadata","x"]`, `["type"]`) — the array disambiguates a key that itself contains a dot, and the first segment selects the root member (`body`/`metadata`/`type`/`authority`), consistent with root-addressed `patch`/`selector`. NOT a dotted string.
/// normative: Wire encoding — every protocol DU is INTERNALLY-TAGGED with a `kind` discriminator = the arm name in kebab-case (the convention of [[core.lumen#Outcome]], [[core.lumen#ChangeEvent]]), NEVER externally-tagged (`{"compare":{…}}`). A condition is `{"kind":"compare","field":["body","status"],"op":"eq","value":…}`; a flat `Filter` is a JSON array of them. list and the stdlib `query` share this one encoding. On the query string the baseline floor every scope accepts is the URL-encoded JSON of this `Filter` array — for both `?where=` (list) and `?precondition=` (the write state guard). For `?where=` ONLY, a scope MAY ALSO advertise ([[core.lumen#ListCaps]] `compactFilter`) a compact `field op value` dialect (comma-separated conditions, `/`-separated field paths) for the common `compare`/`has`/`missing` subset — a human-typable convenience, never required, with the gnarly arms (`matches`, `contains*`, array-valued `in`) staying JSON or going to the stdlib `query`. `?precondition=` is JSON-only — it is machine-generated, never the compact dialect.
/// normative: v0 `compare` is LEXICAL (string) comparison — a numeric `lt`/`le`/`gt`/`ge` on a value like `priority` orders as text (`"10" < "2"`). Typed comparison by the field's declared type (now that `integer` and `number` are distinguished) is a follow-up, to land consistently across `list` and `query`.
type listCondition = union {
  /// description: Compare a field to a value with a scalar operator (or set membership, `in`).
  compare     { field: FieldPath, op: "eq"|"ne"|"lt"|"le"|"gt"|"ge"|"in", value: any }
  /// description: Match a field's text against a pattern (substring, glob, or regex).
  matches     { field: FieldPath, pattern: string, using: "substring"|"glob"|"regex" }
  /// description: The field is present.
  has         { field: FieldPath }
  /// description: The field is absent.
  missing     { field: FieldPath }
  /// description: A list field contains the value.
  contains    { field: FieldPath, value: any }
  /// description: A list field contains any of the values.
  containsAny { field: FieldPath, values: [ any ] }
  /// description: A list field does not contain the value.
  excludes    { field: FieldPath, value: any }
  /// description: Match the type-path (`type.id`) by prefix (the "is it an issue?" test) or exact.
  isType      { type: RelativeId, match: "exact" | "prefix" = "prefix" }
}

## FieldPath
/// description: A field path as a segment array (disambiguates dotted keys); selector/patch keep JSON Pointer.
type FieldPath = [ string ]

## RelativeId
/// description: A type-path as a local id segment, relative to the scope (the target for isType's prefix/exact match).
type RelativeId is string

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3.2  Export & change notification — the changefeed, then export/import
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The live and bulk faces of the same thing: the changefeed streams every commit
// as a ChangeEvent; export serializes the same representations as a backup. Both
// carry a cut Cursor so a consumer resumes the feed from where a backup ends.

// --- the changefeed: /sys/changes + ChangeEvent ---
// Each Scope has one built-in changefeed Channel (source; pullSchema = ChangeEvent)
// at the well-known id `/sys/changes`. Its events are what create/put/patch/delete
// commit; read it with `pull`.

## ChangeEvent
/// description: Core's report of one committed change to one Resource — Created / Updated / Deleted — or an operation-frame boundary (CallEnter / CallLeave) for an unsafe call. A top-level DU on lifecycle (a relationship change is an Updated to the relationship resource). `subject.rev` is the new (or tombstone) revision. Provenance rides in the post-image where there is one and is explicit where there is not.
/// normative: One commit produces exactly one event per resource changed, however many times the request touched it; a failed request and a no-change request produce no resource event. An event is durable with its change — never committed without its event, never published for a change that did not commit. A change that arrives by replication MUST still appear on the changefeed. The full (post-image) encoding is required of every service; the patch encoding is optional (a reader that meets a patch it cannot apply re-reads the resource).
/// normative: CallEnter / CallLeave bound an operation's frame on the feed — the open carries the operation, the close its outcome. They are emitted for an UNSAFE or unspecified operation only (the `safe` marker on [[core.lumen#Operation]] is the gate); a safe operation makes no change and leaves no frame — its boundaries are read from the Request/Reply pair, which already carries the same callId / cause / transaction and the outcome. So an unsafe operation that changes nothing is still visible — its enter and leave bracket no resource events — while a read leaves the feed untouched.
type ChangeEvent = union {
  Created
  Updated
  Deleted
  CallEnter
  CallLeave
}
// >DB? the delete-provenance shape (provenance per-arm, elided where the post-image
// carries it, explicit on patch/delete) is the proposal you marked "review this shape".
// >DB the wire discriminator for this DU is a `kind` field = the arm identifier
// lowercased (`created` … `call-leave`), NOT the `type` kind-path — deferred to the
// lumen scrub; the tag field is not written here yet.

## ChangeEnvelope
/// description: Fields common to EVERY arm — position, time, and the correlation tokens. No subject: a resource arm adds that via [[core.lumen#ResourceChange]]; a frame arm (CallEnter / CallLeave) has none.
type ChangeEnvelope = {
  /// description: Position in the changefeed, assigned on append.
  ordinal:      Ordinal,
  /// description: When the event committed.
  at:           Timestamp,
  /// description: The commit this event belongs to — and, for a read's frame, the OCC snapshot the operation saw. Always present.
  transaction:  Transaction,
  /// description: The cause this event ran under.
  cause?:       Cause,
  /// description: The parent cause — the cause-tree back-pointer.
  causedBy?:    Cause,
  /// description: The call this event belongs to — its CallId (its own, for a frame arm) (diagnostic-tier).
  callId?:      CallId,
  /// description: The parent call — the call-tree back-pointer (diagnostic-tier).
  calledBy?:    CallId
}

## ResourceChange
/// description: What the resource arms (Created / Updated / Deleted) add to [[core.lumen#ChangeEnvelope]] — the subject and its denormalized type. A frame arm carries none of these.
type ResourceChange = {
  ...ChangeEnvelope,
  /// description: The changed resource — { scope, id, rev }; `rev` is the new (or tombstone) revision.
  subject:           ResourceId,
  /// description: The subject's type path (= its kind, `type.id`), denormalized for filtering.
  subjectType?:      PathString,
  /// description: Who names the subject's type — the package or registry, denormalized. Absent ⇒ ad-hoc.
  subjectAuthority?: Authority
}

## Created
/// description: A resource admitted. Full post-image; its provenance is the representation's provenance.
type Created = {
  ...ResourceChange,
  /// description: The full post-image of the new resource; its provenance is the change's provenance.
  representation: Resource
}

## Updated
/// description: A resource changed. `content` is the orthogonal encoding axis — `full` carries a post-image (provenance rides in it), `patch` is a delta with explicit provenance.
type Updated = {
  ...ResourceChange,
  /// description: The change payload — a full post-image or a patch delta (see [[core.lumen#ChangeContent]]).
  content: ChangeContent
}

## Deleted
/// description: A resource retired — the tombstone. No post-image, so provenance is explicit; `subject.rev` is the tombstone revision.
type Deleted = {
  ...ResourceChange,
  /// description: Explicit provenance for the retirement — there is no post-image to carry it.
  provenance: Provenance
}

## CallEnter
/// description: An operation frame opened — the open boundary of an unsafe call on the changefeed. Carries the operation; its callId / cause / transaction ride the envelope.
type CallEnter = {
  ...ChangeEnvelope,
  /// description: The operation whose frame opened.
  operation: ref<Operation>
}

## CallLeave
/// description: An operation frame closed — the close boundary of an unsafe call. Carries the outcome (success / failure / fault / canceled, with the value); its callId / cause / transaction ride the envelope.
type CallLeave = {
  ...ChangeEnvelope,
  /// description: How the call settled — the domain Result or a non-domain fate, with its value.
  outcome: Outcome<any, any>
}

## ChangeContent
/// description: The change payload for Updated — full-vs-patch, the encoding axis (orthogonal to lifecycle).
type ChangeContent = union {
  full  { representation: Resource }               // a post-image; provenance rides in it
  patch { patch: JsonPatch, provenance: Provenance }    // a PATCH delta; no post-image → provenance explicit
}

// --- the export format (JSON Lines: begin, representations, end) ---
// A begin record, one Resource record per line (literally the result of `get` — a
// full representation, or every revision under `--revisions`), then an end record.
// A reader refuses a file that ends without the end record.

## ExportRecord
/// description: One line of an export. The export is a strict in-order serialization, so a record's POSITION in the stream is its implicit ordinal — the resume key a resumable `import` acks against ([[core.lumen#import]]). No ordinal field is added: the position carries it, and `ExportBegin` already carries the changefeed cut the export was taken at.
type ExportRecord = ExportBegin | Resource | ExportEnd

## ExportBegin
/// description: The opening line — the Scope and the cursor the export was cut at (where a consumer resumes the changefeed). Told apart from a Resource line by `kind`.
type ExportBegin = {
  /// description: The discriminator that tells a begin line from a resource line.
  kind:   "export-begin",
  /// description: The scope this export covers.
  scope:  AbsoluteHttpUrl,
  /// description: The cut the export was taken at — where a consumer resumes the changefeed.
  cursor: Cursor
}

## ExportEnd
/// description: The closing line — a count of the Resource records, so a truncated file (one ending without this line) is detectable and never taken for whole.
/// normative: The completed read is the truncation conformance point: a whole export — a one-shot reader's entire file, or a resumable import AT the call that consumes `ExportEnd` (the ack bearing `complete`) — MUST end with an `ExportEnd`, and a reader that takes a whole export lacking it refuses it (`400`). An intermediate resume chunk is a PREFIX by design: it is acked (`acceptedThrough`, not `complete`) and re-offered from, never refused for want of a terminator.
/// normative: A reader refuses (`400`) an `ExportEnd` whose `count` disagrees with the Resource records it closes over — this is what tells a whole file from two exports concatenated (each ending in its own `ExportEnd`), which the implicit record-ordinal (stream position) cannot distinguish.
type ExportEnd = {
  /// description: The discriminator that marks the closing line.
  kind:  "export-end",
  /// description: The number of resource records — lets a reader detect a truncated file.
  count: integer
}

## export
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "export" }
/// description: Bulk read of a scope as JSONL — current state by default; `--revisions` emits every revision in order (a true backup: createdAt, every committedAt, the previousRevision chain survive). Selection ⊆ Filter. Reads from the authoritative revision store, so it is a backup even when the live changefeed is compacted. Consistent as of a cut Cursor.
/// openapi = { operationId = "export", method = "GET", pathItem = "scope", tags = ["Core"], params = { format = { in = "header", schema = "Transport", required = false } }, success = { status = 200, contentType = "application/jsonl" }, problem = { status = [400] } }
fn export(
    /// description: The scope to back up.
    scope:      Scope,
    /// description: Restrict the export to matching resources (see [[core.lumen#Filter]]).
    where?:     Filter,
    /// description: Project each exported resource.
    select?:    selector,
    /// description: Emit every revision in order (a true backup) instead of current state.
    revisions?: bool
  ) -> snapshot<ExportRecord>

## ImportStrategy
/// description: How `import` merges — strategy and id remap.
type ImportStrategy = {
  /// description: Skip a record already present instead of faulting (idempotent re-import).
  dedup?:      bool,
  /// description: Admit a record whose revision is behind the store's current one.
  allowStale?: bool,
  /// description: Validate and report, but write nothing.
  dryRun?:     bool,
  /// description: Remap incoming ids under this id-root prefix.
  idRoot?:     string
}

## ImportReport
/// description: The import tally — counts and any conflicted records. It is the `tallies` of the final [[core.lumen#ImportAck]] (the call that consumes `ExportEnd`); the old terminal whole-batch report folds here. Also returned directly by pack ops that summarize a bulk load.
type ImportReport = {
  /// description: How many records were written.
  imported:  integer,
  /// description: How many records were skipped as already present (content `dedup`).
  skipped:   integer,
  /// description: Rejected records, each by its id and the reason.
  conflicts: [ { id: ref<any>, reason: string } ]
}

## ImportResult
/// description: The fate of one offered export record, in stream order — mirrors [[core.lumen#PushResult]] with an added hard-reject arm. The record is identified by its stream ordinal (its position in the export).
type ImportResult = union {
  /// description: Applied (written) at this ordinal.
  Accepted   { ordinal: Ordinal }
  /// description: A benign no-op — EITHER an idempotent resume re-offer (ordinal ≤ `acceptedThrough`), OR a content `dedup` skip (the resource is already present). Two distinct mechanisms, one fate (see the import normative notes).
  Deduped    { ordinal: Ordinal }
  /// description: Not processed on this call (backpressure); re-offer the tail from `acceptedThrough + 1` after `retryAfter`.
  Deferred
  /// description: Hard per-record reject — the record was PROCESSED (it counts toward `acceptedThrough`) but not applied; resume never re-offers it.
  Conflicted { ordinal: Ordinal, reason: string }
}

## ImportAck
/// description: What [[core.lumen#import]] returns — the push-style acknowledgement for a resumable, backpressured bulk load, mirroring [[core.lumen#PushAck]]. `acceptedThrough` is the high-water record ordinal PROCESSED by this call; the importer re-offers the deferred tail from `acceptedThrough + 1`. The call that consumes `ExportEnd` sets `complete: true` and carries the final `tallies`; a one-shot (non-resumable) import returns a single ack already `complete`.
type ImportAck = {
  /// description: The high-water record ordinal PROCESSED (applied-or-rejected) by this call; the importer re-offers from `acceptedThrough + 1`.
  acceptedThrough: Ordinal,
  /// description: How many more records the scope will accept right now (admission credit); `0` means hold and retry.
  credit:          integer,
  /// description: How long to wait before re-offering the deferred tail (seconds; maps to HTTP `Retry-After`).
  retryAfter?:     integer,
  /// description: Per offered record, in stream order — applied, deduped, deferred, or conflicted.
  results:         [ImportResult],
  /// description: Present and `true` on the call that consumed `ExportEnd` — the import is whole.
  complete?:       bool,
  /// description: The folded tally ([[core.lumen#ImportReport]]) — authoritative on the final (`complete`) ack.
  tallies:         ImportReport
}

## import
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "import" }
/// description: The bulk write path — put/create under If-Match with id remap; a history file is replayed in commit order to rebuild the chain (history-restore), else it sets current state. Not a filter op. Push-style and resumable: the importer offers the export stream, the scope accepts a prefix and defers the rest under backpressure, acks `acceptedThrough`, and the importer re-offers from `acceptedThrough + 1`. Resume is an advertised capability ([[core.lumen#ImportCaps]] `window`); a minimal scope keeps one-shot import (one ack, already `complete`).
/// normative: `import` is a bulk WRITE (put/create), not a filter/read; a history file is replayed in commit order (history-restore), otherwise it sets current state.
/// normative: Import is RESUMABLE over the export's implicit record ordinals ([[core.lumen#ExportRecord]]). `import` is non-blocking: the scope processes a prefix up to its current admission `credit` (advertised as `window`), marks the remainder `Deferred`, and returns `acceptedThrough` (the high-water ordinal PROCESSED) with `credit` and an optional `retryAfter`. The importer holds the deferred tail and re-offers from `acceptedThrough + 1`; re-offering a record at or below `acceptedThrough` is a benign no-op reported `Deduped`, never a fault. This resume-idempotency is the model's own, not the caller's strategy.
/// normative: `acceptedThrough` is the last PROCESSED record — applied OR rejected — NOT the last success. A `Conflicted` record counts toward `acceptedThrough`, so resume SKIPS it and never re-offers it; a dropped-and-resumed import converges to the same per-record fates as one uninterrupted call.
/// normative: TWO idempotencies ride import and MUST NOT be conflated. Resume-idempotency is protocol-level: a re-offered ordinal ≤ `acceptedThrough` is a no-op, independent of content. Content `dedup` ([[core.lumen#ImportStrategy]]) is a merge strategy: a record whose resource is already present is skipped. Both surface as `Deduped`, but the first is keyed on the stream ordinal and the second on resource presence — they coincide only by accident.
/// normative: Backpressure — an `ImportAck` carrying `credit: 0` and a deferred tail — is soft and temporal, not an error, exactly as [[core.lumen#push]]. It is distinct from `limit-exceeded` (422), the hard rejection of a request that exceeds a static capability bound, fixed by shrinking the request. `dryRun` ([[core.lumen#ImportStrategy]]) is inherently one-shot — nothing durable is written, so nothing can be resumed — and returns a single `complete` ack.
/// openapi = { operationId = "import", method = "POST", pathItem = "scope", tags = ["Core"], success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [400,409,422] } }
command import(
    /// description: The scope to import into.
    scope:     Scope,
    /// description: The export stream to replay — begin, resources, end (see [[core.lumen#ExportRecord]]); a resumed call re-offers from `acceptedThrough + 1`.
    source:    snapshot<ExportRecord>,
    /// description: How to merge, and whether to remap ids (see [[core.lumen#ImportStrategy]]).
    strategy?: ImportStrategy
  ) -> ImportAck

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3.3  Discovery & capabilities — the primitive set, advertised à la carte
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Each primitive is defined once, at its anchor; this is the index, plus the
//   discovery contract that lets a caller NEVER GUESS what a scope offers.
//
//   | primitive  | target      | defined in |
//   | get        | resource    | §1.2       |
//   | list       | scope       | §3.1       |
//   | pull       | channel     | §2.2       |
//   | create     | scope       | §3.1       |
//   | put        | resource    | §3.1       |
//   | patch      | resource    | §3.1       |
//   | delete     | resource    | §3.1       |
//   | revisions  | resource    | §3.1       |
//   | call       | operation   | §2.3       |
//   | cancel     | asyncResult | §2.3       |
//   | push       | channel     | §2.2       |
//   | export     | scope       | §3.2       |
//   | import     | scope       | §3.2       |
//
//   NO TIERS. A scope advertises WHICH primitives it offers, à la carte, through a
//   well-known discovery resource. `get` and discovery are the mandatory floor;
//   every other primitive is present iff advertised. A request for an un-offered
//   primitive is refused `capability-not-supported`; an optional feature is never
//   half-present. Call / cancel (operations) and the stdlib packs (apply = batch,
//   eval = query) advertise under their own packages, not core.
//
//   Well-known resources (addressed by convention):
//     /sys/changes       — the scope's changefeed Channel (source; pullSchema = ChangeEvent)
//     /sys/capabilities  — the scope's discovery resource: `get` it for the scope's
//                          `Capabilities` (below). With `get` and the `GET /`
//                          ServiceDescriptor, it is the floor.

## ServiceDescriptor
/// description: The bounded discovery signpost returned by `GET /` (a scope's base URL) — a THIN envelope that names the protocol and LINKS to the rest, never embedding it. With `get` and `/sys/capabilities`, it is the mandatory floor.
/// normative: `GET /` returns a ServiceDescriptor, not the scope's resource collection — the scope list moved to `/-/list`. The fixed well-known paths ARE the floor and ARE load-bearing: a client MAY hardcode `GET /`, `GET /sys/capabilities` and `GET /bdp/openapi`. The `links` (and their `Link`-header mirror, RFC 8288) are an OPTIONAL relation-following convenience over those paths — the body is authoritative; the `Link` header is a hint and MAY relocate the `base`.
type ServiceDescriptor = {
  /// description: The BDP protocol version this scope speaks — a full SemVer (e.g. `"0.2.3"`); negotiation reads `major.minor.patch`.
  version:   SemVer,
  /// description: The exact spec commit/tag this scope was built against — finer-grained provenance, diagnostic only, never used for compatibility.
  revision?: Rev,
  /// description: The scope's identity, echoed for confirmation. Absent on a base URL that fronts no single scope.
  scope?: BdpScope,
  /// description: The scope's base URL, echoed so a client can confirm the origin it reached.
  base?:  AbsoluteHttpUrl,
  /// description: Typed links to the rest of the service. See [[core.lumen#ServiceLinks]].
  links:  ServiceLinks
}

## ServiceLinks
/// description: The discovery link set — RFC 8631 web-service link relations to the service's other resources. A relation's absence means the service does not offer it. Mirrored in the `Link` header.
/// normative: Relations — `service-desc` → `/bdp/openapi` (the static OpenAPI contract) and `service-meta` → `/sys/capabilities` (live, per-deployment capabilities) are the RFC 8631 registered relations (the OpenAPI location is advertised the traditional way, `Link: </bdp/openapi>; rel="service-desc"`); `service-doc` → human documentation. The changefeed is NOT an IANA relation, so it uses the BDP extension relation URI `tag:bdp.dev,2026:rel/changes` → `/sys/changes` (a bare `changes` token is never emitted in the `Link` header). The split is deliberate: `/bdp/openapi` is the STATIC contract, `/sys/capabilities` is LIVE per-deployment state.
type ServiceLinks = {
  /// description: RFC 8631 `service-desc` — the machine contract (the OpenAPI document, `/bdp/openapi`).
  serviceDesc?: AbsoluteHttpUrl,
  /// description: RFC 8631 `service-meta` — the scope's live `Capabilities` (`/sys/capabilities`).
  serviceMeta?: AbsoluteHttpUrl,
  /// description: RFC 8631 `service-doc` — human-readable documentation.
  serviceDoc?:  AbsoluteHttpUrl,
  /// description: `changes` (BDP extension relation `tag:bdp.dev,2026:rel/changes`) — the scope's changefeed Channel (`/sys/changes`).
  changes?:     AbsoluteHttpUrl
}

## Capabilities
/// description: What a scope advertises through discovery — read it by `get` on the well-known `/sys/capabilities` resource. Its primitive capabilities are partitioned by package (`core` is the base; other packages advertise their own), à la carte: a primitive is supported iff its capability is present. Scope-wide traits that are not per-primitive live in `scope`.
/// normative: Every scope offers `get` and a readable `/sys/capabilities`. A caller reads it once and relies on it: a primitive is supported iff its capability is present; a request that needs an un-offered primitive is refused `capability-not-supported` (the enforce side of this advertisement), and an optional feature is never half-present.
type Capabilities = {
  /// description: The base (`core`) package's advertised primitive capabilities — what every BDP caller can use by name.
  core:   CoreCapabilities,
  /// description: Scope-wide traits that are neither per-primitive nor pack-specific — storage classes and the revision model.
  scope:  ScopeCapabilities,
  /// description: Other packages' advertised capabilities, keyed by package path (the query / batch / domain packs). The package system fills these in; `core` is the base.
  packs?: { ...: any }
}

## CoreCapabilities
/// description: The `core` package's advertised primitives, à la carte — a primitive is supported iff its field is present, and its value carries that primitive's limits. `get` is always present (the floor). Each limit record is the advertise side of a dual: the matching `limit-exceeded` (422) is the enforce side.
type CoreCapabilities = {
  /// description: Always present — point reads are the floor. See [[core.lumen#GetCaps]].
  get:        GetCaps,
  /// description: Present iff `list` is offered. See [[core.lumen#ListCaps]].
  list?:      ListCaps,
  /// description: Present iff `pull` is offered (the changefeed and declared streams). See [[core.lumen#PullCaps]].
  pull?:      PullCaps,
  /// description: Present iff `create` is offered. See [[core.lumen#CreateCaps]].
  create?:    CreateCaps,
  /// description: Present iff `put` is offered. See [[core.lumen#PutCaps]].
  put?:       PutCaps,
  /// description: Present iff `patch` is offered. See [[core.lumen#PatchCaps]].
  patch?:     PatchCaps,
  /// description: Present iff `delete` is offered. See [[core.lumen#DeleteCaps]].
  delete?:    DeleteCaps,
  /// description: Present iff prior revisions are addressable (`revisions`); offered only by a `versioned` scope. See [[core.lumen#RevisionsCaps]].
  revisions?: RevisionsCaps,
  /// description: Present iff `export` is offered. See [[core.lumen#ExportCaps]].
  export?:    ExportCaps,
  /// description: Present iff `import` is offered. See [[core.lumen#ImportCaps]].
  import?:    ImportCaps,
  /// description: Present iff `push` is offered. See [[core.lumen#PushCaps]].
  push?:      PushCaps
}

## ScopeCapabilities
/// description: A scope's scope-wide traits — the capabilities that are neither per-primitive nor pack-specific. Storage classes, the revision model, and whether aliases are supported live here.
/// normative: THE SCOPE-WIDE TRAITS DO NOT COMBINE FREELY. `versioned` ([[core.lumen#RevisionModel]]) with `reusable` ([[core.lumen#IdModel]]) is FORBIDDEN, and a scope MUST NOT advertise that pair. Such a scope would keep a tombstone revision and then re-admit the id, so [[core.lumen#revisions]] at that id would answer with the history of TWO unrelated resources — contradicting its own definition, "the history of one resource" — and the `previousRevision` chain, the basis for compensation, would walk across the boundary into a resource the caller never named. A pinned `get` under `?revision=` would likewise resolve one id to two different resources. The prohibition is a consequence of what a revision chain MEANS, not of any implementation's behaviour. So `versioned` ⇒ `permanent`, and equivalently `reusable` ⇒ `unversioned`. The other three combinations are legal and each has its own read of `delete`'s `404` ([[core.lumen#delete]]): `versioned`+`permanent`, `unversioned`+`permanent`, `unversioned`+`reusable`.
type ScopeCapabilities = {
  /// description: Which persistence classes the scope can honor — a set over `Durable` / `Ephemeral`. A resource whose class is not honored cannot be hosted. `Intrinsic` is universal and never appears here.
  storageClasses: [StorageClass],
  /// description: Whether the scope mints revisions, scope-wide. See [[core.lumen#RevisionModel]].
  revisionModel:  RevisionModel,
  /// description: Whether a retired id is permanently spent, scope-wide. See [[core.lumen#IdModel]].
  idModel:        IdModel,
  /// description: Present iff the scope supports alias resources and the scope-root overlay. See [[core.lumen#Alias]], [[core.lumen#AliasCaps]].
  aliases?:       AliasCaps,
  /// description: Present and `true` iff the scope accepts the OPTIONAL body-form spill ([[core.lumen#Envelope]]) for an over-budget read or write guard; absent/false ⇒ an over-budget request is refused `request-too-large`. There is no advertised URL-byte budget — intermediaries, not the origin, impose the limit, so a client is reactive to `414`/`431`/`400`-by-length regardless.
  bodyFormSpill?: bool
}

## AliasCaps
/// description: Advertised for `aliases` — its presence signals that `Alias` resources under `sys/aliases/` and the scope-root overlay are active. An alias is always single-hop and same-scope (no chains); any further limits (e.g. a cap on aliases per target) ride here.
type AliasCaps = { ...: any }

## RevisionModel
/// description: A scope's revision model — a scope-wide trait, stated outright (no implicit default).
/// normative: `versioned` ⇒ the scope mints revisions; write refs are `pinned`, pinned-revision (time-travel) `get` resolves, and the changefeed carries revisions. It also constrains the id model: `versioned` REQUIRES `permanent`, since a revision chain may not span two resources under one id ([[core.lumen#ScopeCapabilities]]). `unversioned` ⇒ the scope mints no revisions; `rev` is absent everywhere, write refs relax to `unpinned`, and every revision-dependent behaviour relaxes scope-wide. `unversioned` leaves the id model free — both `permanent` and `reusable` are legal with it.
type RevisionModel = "versioned" | "unversioned"

## IdModel
/// description: A scope's id model — a scope-wide trait, stated outright (no implicit default). Whether an id retired by `delete` is permanently spent or may be minted again.
/// normative: `permanent` ⇒ a retired id is never reused. An unpinned inbound reference to it resolves to the tombstone and faults `410 Gone`; `create` at that id faults `identity-taken`, and so does a `put` under `ifAbsent`, which asserts the id is free; a bare `put` instead TARGETS the retired resource and faults `410` — rooting cannot discriminate, since every `put` is id-rooted, so the guard is what does ([[core.lumen#put]]). Where the scope is also `versioned`, a client that wants to read what the resource WAS pins a revision; the id alone only reaches the tombstone. `reusable` ⇒ a retired id MAY be minted again, and REQUIRES `unversioned` ([[core.lumen#ScopeCapabilities]]). `get` of a retired id faults `404`, a later `create` at that id succeeds, and an unpinned inbound reference MAY therefore resolve to an UNRELATED resource. Pinning is NOT the remedy here and must not be offered as one: a `reusable` scope mints no revisions, so there is no pinned form of the reference to hold. On such a scope a reference cannot be made to survive a delete at all, and a client that needs one must carry identity the scope's ids do not supply.
/// normative: The id model is declared, never inferred, and a scope states it outright like its revision model. `permanent` is not a floor every provider can hold: a scope projecting an existing system whose id allocation it does not control is expected to state `reusable` rather than claim a guarantee it cannot keep. Stating `reusable` is conformant; claiming `permanent` and reusing an id is not.
/// normative: A scope declaring `reusable` and offering `delete` MUST advertise `precondition` ON `delete` — `precondition` true in [[core.lumen#DeleteCaps]]. It cannot discharge this with `ifRevision`: `reusable` requires `unversioned` ([[core.lumen#ScopeCapabilities]]), so no revision exists for `If-Match` to carry, and `precondition` is the only guard such a scope can offer at all. That it pins by CONTENT rather than by revision is what makes the requirement reachable: a projection with no revision identity can still satisfy it. The guard must be on `delete` specifically, not merely somewhere among the writes — the hazard is a `delete` RETRY against a recycled id, so a scope guarding `put` while leaving `delete` unguarded would satisfy a looser rule and remain unsafe. Without a guard a client has no safe way to retry a `delete` at all, and cannot learn that from a failure, because the unsafe retry SUCCEEDS.
type IdModel = "permanent" | "reusable"

## GetCaps
/// description: Limits advertised for `get`.
type GetCaps = {
  /// description: Whether a `select` projection is honored on a read.
  selector: bool
}

## ListCaps
/// description: Limits advertised for `list`.
type ListCaps = {
  /// description: The list-condition operators the low-bar filter supports.
  conditions: [ConditionKind],
  /// description: Whether a `select` projection is honored.
  selector:   bool,
  /// description: Whether server-side sort is offered.
  sort:       bool,
  /// description: Whether the compact `?where=` dialect is accepted, in addition to the URL-encoded JSON Filter floor every scope accepts.
  compactFilter: bool,
  /// description: The maximum page size, when capped.
  maxLimit?:  integer
}

## ConditionKind
/// description: One operator in the low-bar `list` filter. The richer filter / query language is a corelib (query-pack) capability, not core.
type ConditionKind = "compare" | "matches" | "has" | "missing" | "contains" | "containsAny" | "excludes" | "isType"

## PullCaps
/// description: Limits advertised for `pull` (the changefeed and declared streams).
type PullCaps = {
  /// description: Whether live `sse` transport is offered.
  sse:      bool,
  /// description: Whether bulk `jsonl` transport is offered.
  jsonl:    bool,
  /// description: Whether a `where` filter is honored on the stream.
  filter:   bool,
  /// description: Whether a `select` projection is honored on each item.
  selector: bool
}

## CreateCaps
/// description: Limits advertised for `create`.
type CreateCaps = {
  /// description: Whether the server will allocate the id (no `requestedId`).
  serverAllocatesId: bool,
  /// description: Whether a caller-proposed id (`requestedId`) is honored.
  clientProposedId:  bool
}

## PutCaps
/// description: Limits advertised for `put` — which write guards it honors. The `precondition` operator set is ⊆ [[core.lumen#ListCaps]] `conditions`.
type PutCaps = {
  /// description: Whether the `ifRevision` (If-Match) guard is honored.
  ifRevision:   bool,
  /// description: Whether the `ifAbsent` (If-None-Match: *) create-if-absent guard is honored.
  ifAbsent:     bool,
  /// description: Whether the `?precondition=` state guard is honored.
  precondition: bool
}

## PatchCaps
/// description: Limits advertised for `patch`. The `precondition` operator set is ⊆ [[core.lumen#ListCaps]] `conditions`.
type PatchCaps = {
  /// description: Whether the `ifRevision` (If-Match) precondition is honored.
  ifRevision:   bool,
  /// description: Whether the `?precondition=` state guard is honored.
  precondition: bool
}

## DeleteCaps
/// description: Limits advertised for `delete`. The `precondition` operator set is ⊆ [[core.lumen#ListCaps]] `conditions`.
type DeleteCaps = {
  /// description: Whether the `ifRevision` (If-Match) precondition is honored.
  ifRevision:   bool,
  /// description: Whether the `?precondition=` state guard is honored.
  precondition: bool
}

## RevisionsCaps
/// description: Presence advertises that prior revisions are addressable (`revisions`); offered only by a `versioned` scope.
type RevisionsCaps = { }

## ExportCaps
/// description: Limits advertised for `export`.
type ExportCaps = {
  /// description: Whether the export can include prior revisions (history), not only the current state.
  revisions: bool
}

## ImportCaps
/// description: Limits advertised for `import`.
type ImportCaps = {
  /// description: Whether importing prior revisions (history restore) is honored.
  historyRestore: bool,
  /// description: The admission window — how many export records the scope will accept before backpressure, when bounded. Its PRESENCE advertises resumable/backpressured import ([[core.lumen#ImportAck]]); absent ⇒ one-shot import only (a single `complete` ack, no resume). Mirrors [[core.lumen#PushCaps]] `window`.
  window?:        integer
}

## PushCaps
/// description: Limits advertised for `push`.
type PushCaps = {
  /// description: The maximum number of events accepted in one batch, when capped.
  maxBatch?: integer,
  /// description: The admission window — how many events the channel will take before backpressure, when bounded.
  window?:   integer
}



// #############################################################################
// Appendix A. Worked examples (wire representations)
// #############################################################################
// --- authoring (Lumen) ---
//   type Issue is Bead  { title: string, status: "open" | "closed" }   // → "issue"
//   type Task  is Issue { estimate?: number }                          // → "issue/task"
//   type Bug   is Issue { severity: "low" | "high" }                   // → "issue/bug"
//   type Memory     { statement: string }
//   type Spec       is Memory     { status: "draft" | "accepted" }     // → "memory/spec"
//   type Constraint is Memory     { applies: "always" | "conditional" }// → "memory/constraint"
//   type Policy     is Constraint { enforcement: "advisory" | "block" }// → "memory/constraint/policy"
//
// --- a bead on the wire (a task) ---
//   { id:        { id: "4821", rev: "r7" },                              // scope absent ⇒ local
//     authority: { scope: "uri", id: "https://pkg.gastownhall.dev/beads", rev: "sha256:9f…" },
//     type:      "issue/task",
//     body:      { title: "Wire the changefeed", status: "open", estimate: 3 },   // firewalled
//     metadata: {}, provenance: { … }, createdAt: "2026-11-16T18:04:12Z",
//     storageClass: Durable }
//   // kind (derived) = type.id = "issue/task"
//
// --- a content resource on the wire (text/plain) — a bare Resource ---
//   { id:        { id: "assets/readme.txt", rev: "r1" },                 // scope absent ⇒ local
//     authority: { scope: "uri", id: "https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types" },
//     type:      "text/plain",
//     body:      "Hello, world.\n",                                      // raw, flat — no firewall
//     metadata: {}, provenance: { … }, createdAt: "2026-11-02T09:00:00Z",
//     storageClass: Durable }

// #############################################################################
// Appendix B. The `///` backend metadata vocabulary
// #############################################################################
// One namespaced record per backend; the single top-level key is the namespace —
// no dotted keys. `title:`/`description:` are plain strings consumed by every
// backend. Backends: schema bundle, OpenAPI, Go, reference, CLI.
//   bdp        — protocol markers: { kind = "primitive", primitive = "get", id = "<uri>" }
//   openapi    — projection control: { operationId, method, pathItem, tags, params,
//                paramGroups, request, success, problem, responses, responseHeaders }
//   jsonschema — base-type refinements: { format }, { pattern }, { minLength }, { closed }
//   doc        — reference-rendering mode: { style = "http" | "rpc" }
//   normative  — a conformance statement, lowered to a numbered MUST/SHOULD line.
//
// requestContext → BDP-Cause / BDP-Provenance / BDP-Metadata request headers;
// replyContext   → BDP-Cause / BDP-Transaction / BDP-Metadata reply headers.


// #############################################################################
// Appendix C. Lowering — Lumen → JSON Schema & OpenAPI (the isomorphism)
// #############################################################################
// The lowering is deterministic and structure-preserving: a Lumen declaration and
// its JSON Schema are the same shape. The one thing that does not round-trip is
// nominal identity — two structurally-equal `type`s are distinct in Lumen but
// identical in JSON Schema (which is structural). So the map is an isomorphism on
// structure; identity is the quotient it factors out. On the wire a Resource
// carries only `type` (a path), never its Lumen type name.
//
// --- types → JSON Schema ----------------------------------------------------
//   Lumen                             JSON Schema
//   --------------------------------  ---------------------------------------------
//   ## Name                           a named schema (component; $id = Name)
//   /// description: …                "description"
//   /// jsonschema = { k = v }        merged as keyword k (format / pattern / …)
//   type X is string                  { "type": "string" }  (+ refinements)
//   type X is number                  { "type": "number" }
//   type X = Y                        { "$ref": "#/…/Y" }         (transparent alias)
//   type X { a: T, b?: U }            { "type":"object", "properties":{a,b},
//                                       "required":["a"], "additionalProperties":false }
//   b?   (optional field)             omitted from "required"
//   { ...: any }                      { "type":"object", "additionalProperties":true }
//   [ T ]                             { "type":"array", "items": T }
//   "open" | "closed"                 { "enum": ["open","closed"] }
//   A | B            (type union)     { "oneOf": [A, B] }
//   = v              (default)        "default": v
//   type X is Y { … }  (refinement)   { "allOf": [ {$ref Y}, {narrowing} ] }
//                                       — flattened to the narrowed schema by the resolver
//   union { Tag {f} … }  (closed DU)  { "oneOf": [ {tagged arm}, … ],
//                                       "discriminator": { "propertyName": "<tag>" } }
//   union { Ref  Tag {…} }  (mixed)   oneOf of the $ref'd schema + the tagged arms
//   open union (Outcome, IdentityBasis)  same oneOf, no closed/unevaluated gate —
//                                       a reader MUST tolerate an unrecognized arm
//   ref<T>                            { "$ref": "#/…/ResourceRef" }   (T erases)
//   pinned<T> / unpinned<T>           ResourceRef with "rev" required / null
//   Resource { fields }               Resource with "body" = the fields' schema (firewall)
//
// --- operations → OpenAPI ---------------------------------------------------
//   A `## op` carrying /// bdp = { kind = "primitive" } + /// openapi = { … }
//   lowers to one path item + operation:
//
//   /// openapi key         OpenAPI
//   ----------------------  -------------------------------------------------------
//   method                  the HTTP method
//   pathItem                the path (resource / scope / channel / operation / asyncResult)
//   operationId, tags       operationId, tags
//   params { in = query }   a query parameter        (e.g. ?revision=, ?mode=)
//   params { in = header }  a header parameter       (If-Match, Accept)
//   request { contentType } requestBody media type   (json-patch+json, jsonl)
//   success { status }      the 2xx response; body = the op's return type
//   problem { status:[…] }  each 4xx/5xx response; body = Problem (application/problem+json)
//   responses [ … ]         explicit per-status responses (call: 200 / 201 / 409 / 202)
//   responseHeaders         response headers         (Location)
//   fn   (pure / safe)      a safe method (GET-shaped); no state change
//   command                 a mutating method
//
//   Counting the fault surface — read BOTH response forms. Twelve of the thirteen
//   primitives declare their 4xx/5xx rows through `problem { status = [ … ] }`, and
//   `call` alone uses `responses [ … ]`, because its statuses carry distinct bodies.
//   A counter that reads only the shorthand silently drops `call`'s 409 — its only
//   >=400 row — and undercounts the surface by one (51 where the answer is 52). The
//   explicit form is NOT an inconsistency to normalize away: the shorthand asserts
//   "this status carries a Problem", and `call/409` does not.
//
//   So `call/409` is the ONLY >=400 response in this binding whose body is not a
//   Problem: it is `application/json` + `Reply`, against `application/problem+json`
//   + `Problem` everywhere else. A conformance case for it asserts a Reply, and the
//   response content type is the discriminator — machine-checkable, no prose needed.
//   A second non-Problem >=400 response would make this a class rather than an
//   exception, and should be ruled on rather than assumed Problem-shaped.
//
// --- normative & context → prose / headers ----------------------------------
//   /// normative: …        one numbered MUST / SHOULD / MAY line in the generated
//                           reference, plus an x-normative note on the lowered schema/op.
//   BDP-* context           lowers to request/reply headers (Appendix B), never to
//                           the URL or body — the body carries the value, the headers
//                           the context.


// #############################################################################
// Appendix D. The HTTP/BDP binding
// #############################################################################
// BDP is an HTTP protocol; this binding is normative. core.lumen is the home of
// all normative material, HTTP included (bdp.md is the overview / transclusion
// shell). The route table in §13.1 and the per-primitive `openapi` annotations
// lower to a generated OpenAPI document (`/bdp/openapi`); this appendix states
// the parts that span operations — the URL space, discovery, write guards,
// read projection, headers, status codes and Problem codes.
//
// --- D.1 the URL space & the action fence -----------------------------------
//   Three parts (see [[core.lumen#PathString]] and the §13.1 route table):
//     • an ID space — a resource is `/{id}`, {id} a free, slash-bearing string.
//     • noun roots — `/sys/*` in-band system RESOURCES (get-addressable beads:
//       `/sys/changes`, `/sys/capabilities`, `/sys/aliases/*`); `/bdp/*` the
//       protocol's own fixed routes & artifacts (`/bdp/openapi` — static only).
//     • the ACTION FENCE `/-/` — a bare `-` segment separates a resource id
//       (left) from a protocol action or projection (right): `/{id}/-/revisions`,
//       `/{opPath}/-/call`, `/{asyncResultId}/-/cancel`; at the scope root the
//       left side is empty, so `/-/list`, `/-/create`.
//   The fence, not a `:verb` suffix, carries actions: an id is a free string, a
//   `:` has URL-reference scheme ambiguity, and real-world ids already bear colons.
//   PARSE: match the fence on the RAW path (pre-decode) as the LAST `/-/`; validate
//   the right side against the closed, fence-free action set (else 404). No id segment
//   may be a bare `-`: a literal hyphen-only segment is percent-encoded (`%2D`) and a
//   write minting an unencoded one is refused, so `%2D` never forges the fence.
//   Scope bulk actions `export`/`import` ride the fence too: `GET /-/export`,
//   `POST /-/import`. `/bdp/*` holds static artifacts only.
//
// --- D.2 discovery (the floor) ----------------------------------------------
//   GET /                → ServiceDescriptor ([[core.lumen#ServiceDescriptor]]) —
//                          a thin signpost: { bdp, scope?, base?, links }. The
//                          `links` repeat in a `Link` header (RFC 8288).
//   GET /sys/capabilities → the scope's live Capabilities (a `get` on a bead).
//   GET /bdp/openapi     → the static OpenAPI contract.
//   The fixed paths ARE load-bearing: a client MAY hardcode `GET /`,
//   `GET /sys/capabilities`, `GET /bdp/openapi`. Relations are an OPTIONAL convenience
//   mirroring them, in `links` and a `Link` response header (RFC 8288, body authoritative):
//   `service-desc` → `/bdp/openapi` and `service-meta` → `/sys/capabilities` are the
//   RFC 8631 registered relations (the OpenAPI location is advertised the traditional way,
//   `Link: </bdp/openapi>; rel="service-desc"`); `service-doc` → docs; the changefeed uses
//   the BDP extension relation `tag:bdp.dev,2026:rel/changes` → `/sys/changes` (never a bare
//   `changes` token). The mandatory floor is `get`, `GET /`, and a readable `/sys/capabilities`.
//
// --- D.3 write guards (PRECONDITIONS — request line, never the body) --------
//   A guard says what must be true to proceed; it is request metadata, carried
//   by a header or the query string, NEVER the payload. So a `put` body is only
//   ever the resource and a `patch` body only ever the JSON Patch. Four guards:
//     create    fail-if-exists is create's nature → `identity-taken` (409).
//     If-Match: "<Rev>"   revision / OCC guard (put/patch/delete) → stale ⇒
//                         `revision-mismatch` (412). `<Rev>` in any header is the
//                         double-quoted strong entity-tag (RFC 9110 §8.8.3).
//     If-None-Match: *    create-if-absent (put) → present ⇒ `identity-taken` (409 —
//                         one id-taken status, shared with create).
//     ?precondition=<Filter>  STATE guard (put/patch/delete) → the current
//                         resource must satisfy the flat-AND [[core.lumen#Filter]]
//                         (the same grammar `list.where` uses, projected onto the
//                         query string) → fails ⇒ `precondition-failed` (412).
//   A `precondition` too large for the URL budget MAY spill to an OPTIONAL, advertised
//   ([[core.lumen#ScopeCapabilities]] `bodyFormSpill`) `query`-tier body-form
//   (`POST /{id}/-/{put|patch|delete}`, media type `application/vnd.bdp.envelope+json`,
//   body = [[core.lumen#Envelope]]: `representation` for put — the FULL rep, never a bare
//   body — `patch` for patch, `precondition?` throughout) — the ONE place a write envelope
//   appears; the `If-Match`/`If-None-Match` guards keep riding their headers. A scope that
//   does not advertise spill refuses an over-budget guard with `request-too-large`.
//   Direct `DELETE` has no body and carries its guard on the request line; an over-budget
//   delete guard MAY still use the advertised POST spill (an `Envelope` with `precondition`
//   only, no body).
//
// --- D.4 reads: projection & the POST spillover -----------------------------
//   `get` `?include=/?omit=/?expand=` (or the full `?select=`), and `list` `?where=`
//   + the selector params, project onto the query string (the selector lowers to the
//   CLI-matching params; the flat-AND Filter lowers to URL-encoded JSON, or an advertised
//   compact dialect), giving a projected read its own cacheable URL. When the query string
//   overruns the URL budget, the read MAY re-send under the fence as a body-form carrying
//   the `ReadRequest`: where `bodyFormSpill` is advertised a service answers `POST`
//   (`/-/list`, `/{id}/-/get`) and MAY answer the draft HTTP `QUERY` method. A body-form
//   read is SAFE and app-idempotent (it mutates nothing); it is not cached by default, so
//   a client prefers the query-string form when it fits. There is NO advertised URL-byte
//   budget: the limit is imposed by intermediaries (proxies/CDNs/browsers), not the origin,
//   so a client reacts to `414`/`431`/`400`-by-length (and MAY spill proactively above a
//   conservative built-in threshold) regardless of what the origin would accept.
//
// --- D.5 request context (headers — never the URL or body) ------------------
//   BDP-Cause        a bare cause token
//   BDP-Provenance   the caller's provenance part, as JSON (ASCII, escaped)
//   BDP-Metadata     per-request metadata, as JSON
//   Rules: one header per member; each appears at most once; header names use
//   hyphens, never underscores. The three together have a floor of 4096 bytes a
//   Scope MUST accept (its max is advertised); oversize ⇒ `request-too-large`,
//   never truncated. A browser EventStream reader sends no request context —
//   allowed, since every member is optional.
//
// --- D.6 reply context (response headers) -----------------------------------
//   BDP-Cause        always present
//   BDP-Transaction  present only where the request made exactly one commit
//   BDP-Metadata     per-reply metadata
//   Link             discovery relations (RFC 8288), mirroring `links` — on `GET /` and,
//                    for `service-desc`, advertisable on any response
//   ETag             the resource's `Rev` as a STRONG entity-tag, double-quoted
//                    (`ETag: "<Rev>"`), so `If-Match: "<Rev>"` is a conformant ETag check
//   Content-Location the canonical id of the returned representation — on an alias rewrite
//                    (it names the target) and on a body-form (POST-tunneled) read/write.
//                    A cache keys on the REQUEST target URI, not on this header; an unsafe
//                    request invalidates the named URI per RFC 9111 §4.4
//   Location         the new URL on `create` / a creating `call` / an async accept
//   The body is the value alone; the context rides in the headers.
//
// --- D.7 content types ------------------------------------------------------
//   application/json              a value / one windowed page; a ServiceDescriptor
//   application/jsonl             bulk stream (pull / list / export; push / import body)
//   text/event-stream            live pull (SSE); the SSE event id is the cursor, so a
//                                reconnect's `Last-Event-ID` resumes exactly as `after`
//   application/json-patch+json  the direct `patch` body (RFC 6902). REQUIRED means
//                                PRESENT-OR-FAULT: a direct `patch` whose `Content-Type` names
//                                another media type, OR OMITS THE HEADER, faults `415`. Absence
//                                is not the lenient case — a requirement that bites only when
//                                you declare something else can be evaded by declaring nothing,
//                                and an omission must never downgrade a guarantee. The
//                                guarantee here is that RFC 7396 merge-patch is never silently
//                                applied; one mistake gets one fault, so a wrong OR missing
//                                media type is `415` rather than 415-or-422 depending on which
//                                way the client erred.
//   application/vnd.bdp.envelope+json  the OPTIONAL body-form spill body ([[core.lumen#Envelope]]):
//                                a POST `/{id}/-/{put|patch|delete}` carrying an over-budget guard
//   application/problem+json     every failing body (RFC 9457 Problem)
//
// --- D.8 status codes -------------------------------------------------------
//   200  get / put / patch / list / revisions / pull success; call → outcome success
//   201  create success, and a call whose Operation `creates` — Location: the new URL
//   202  call ?mode=async — Location: the AsyncResult URL; body: no outcome yet
//   400  `malformed-request` / `invalid-parameter` — a malformed or invalid request: a bad
//        body (unparseable JSON, a patch that is not a JSON-Patch array, a truncated export)
//        or an invalid parameter value. DISTINCT from `422` (a request exceeding an advertised
//        capability bound — `limit-exceeded` — fixed by shrinking). Both `malformed-request`
//        and `invalid-parameter` resolve here.
//   409  call → a declared failure (ruled); any id collision — `create` fail-if-exists OR
//        `put` + `If-None-Match: *` (`identity-taken`); also some Problems
//   412  a failed guard on put / patch / delete — a stale `If-Match` (`revision-mismatch`)
//        or a `precondition` the resource does not satisfy (`precondition-failed`). A
//        present `If-None-Match: *` collision is NOT 412: it is `identity-taken` (409).
//   413  CLIENT-REACTIVE, never origin-declared (`request-too-large`): a request refused as too
//        large. NO operation declares it — ruling D undeclared it from `put`/`patch`/`delete` —
//        because the threshold it would fire at is advertised nowhere. The URL-guard budget is
//        undeclared, and the request-context max promised in D.5 has no capability field to carry
//        it, while D.5's 4096-byte floor is a size a Scope MUST ACCEPT. A client handles this
//        reactively; no portable conformance case can construct the boundary.
//   414  CLIENT-REACTIVE, never origin-declared: the read's query string exceeds the URL budget —
//        retry as a body-form POST. NO operation declares it — ruling D undeclared it from
//        `get`/`list`/`revisions`/`export` — because the limit is imposed by intermediaries
//        (proxies/CDNs/browsers), not the origin, so the origin is not the conformance point and
//        cannot be made to emit one at any length; intermediaries may instead surface it as `431`
//        or a length-related `400`.
//   415  a direct `patch` body whose media type is not `application/json-patch+json` (a
//        body-form patch under `application/vnd.bdp.envelope+json` is exempt)
//   422  a request exceeding an advertised capability bound — a batch over `maxBatch`,
//        or a guard past a scope limit (`limit-exceeded`); fixed by shrinking the request
//   404  get of an absent resource; an unknown action to the right of the `/-/` fence
//   405  pull / push on the wrong channel direction; patch where a Scope offers no patch
//   410  get of a pruned / erased resource; pull below the retention floor (cursor-expired)
//   4xx/5xx  any fault — the body is the Problem, carried at the Problem's own status
//
// --- D.9 Problem codes (BDP extension member `code`; stable `type` URIs) -----
//   `code`/`retry` are BDP extension members, not RFC 9457 core; each code has a stable
//   problem-type URI `tag:bdp.dev,2026:problem/<code>` carried in the Problem `type` member.
//   malformed-request · invalid-parameter · unauthenticated · forbidden ·
//   resource-not-found · resource-pruned · resource-erased · revision-mismatch ·
//   precondition-failed · identity-taken · validation-failed · type-not-installed ·
//   cursor-expired · request-too-large · limit-exceeded · rate-limited ·
//   temporarily-unavailable · capability-not-supported · callable-not-found ·
//   callable-unavailable · mode-not-supported · mode-refused


// #############################################################################
// Appendix E. Conformance — what a Core service MUST provide
// #############################################################################
// A conformance suite is built from this spec, not from any implementation. Each
// `/// normative:` line above is a conformance point; the list below gathers the
// store-wide guarantees (harvested from the prose Core §10).
//
// These guarantees are NOT unconditional, and reading them as such is a known trap: a
// guarantee naming a primitive binds a store only where that primitive is offered, and one
// naming a revision or a retired id is conditioned by the scope's declared models
// ([[core.lumen#ScopeCapabilities]]). The conditions are written into the entries below that
// carry them. A suite that derives an unconditional obligation from this list will fail a
// conformant store on a guarantee its declared models never promised.
//
// So the count of this list is not its shape. Fourteen entries, and FIVE of them are
// conditional: 1, 5 and 14 bind only under a model the scope declares, while 6, 12 and 14 bind
// only where the primitive is offered (14 carries both gates). The remaining nine bind every
// store. Do not partition this list as "eleven unconditional and three gated" — that count is
// only reachable by reading "unconditional" as "not model-conditioned", which silently drops
// the offering gate on 6 and 12.
//
// A suite CITING any of the five is not thereby testing it — the citation must carry the GATE
// the entry names, or the case asserts against scopes the guarantee was never addressed to. A
// case whose precondition is that one of these guarantees is false is testing the relaxation,
// which is sound, and is not evidence about the guarantee.
//
// A conformant store keeps:
//  1. A stable identity per resource — never changed, and under `permanent`
//     ([[core.lumen#IdModel]]) never reused; opaque local part. Under `reusable` a retired id
//     MAY be minted again, which is what that model declares.
//  2. A type per resource — the (authority, type) pair, frozen at inception.
//  3. A body and metadata it stores and does NOT interpret.
//  4. A storage class per resource — Durable | Ephemeral | Intrinsic; whether
//     revisions are kept is the scope's revisionModel, not a per-resource field.
//  5. Under `versioned` ([[core.lumen#RevisionModel]]), a revision per resource that changes
//     exactly when its state changes. Under `unversioned` no revision is minted and `rev` is
//     absent everywhere, so this guarantee is vacuous rather than violated.
//  6. History where offered — prior revisions addressable; offered or not, discoverable.
//  7. A transaction marker shared by everything committed together.
//  8. A cause minted once per incoming request, stamped on every effect it sets off.
//  9. A durable change record per resource per commit — none for a failed or no-change request.
// 10. Provenance saved with the change, immutable, never invented.
// 11. A changefeed at /sys/changes carrying every commit (replication included).
// 12. Where `export` is offered, an export consistent with itself, with the cursor that
//     matches it.
// 13. An authorization view — a hidden resource reads exactly as a missing one.
// 14. Where `delete` is offered, deletion that does not erase — under `permanent` the id stays
//     accounted for, and where the scope is also `versioned` the tombstone revision is kept.
//     A `reusable` scope retains neither record ([[core.lumen#delete]]); that is the declared
//     model, not a breach of this guarantee.
//
// Discovery advertises, per scope: the primitives offered; whether history / patch /
// push are offered; which storage classes `create` accepts; which invocation modes are
// refused; the max request-context size; and where the changefeed is. A request that
// needs an un-offered capability is refused `capability-not-supported`; an optional
// feature is never half-present.
// =============================================================================
